WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.116 --> 00:02.356 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference September 16,

00:02.356 --> 00:03.496 align:middle
2020 CHAIR POWELL.

00:03.656 --> 00:04.076 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:04.276 --> 00:06.796 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:06.796 --> 00:08.876 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:08.876 --> 00:12.316 align:middle
that Congress has given
us-maximum employment

00:12.536 --> 00:13.366 align:middle
and price stability.

00:13.926 --> 00:16.616 align:middle
Since the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:16.996 --> 00:20.086 align:middle
we have taken forceful
actions to provide some relief

00:20.086 --> 00:23.766 align:middle
and stability, to ensure that
the recovery will be as strong

00:23.766 --> 00:27.406 align:middle
as possible, and to limit
lasting damage to the economy.

00:28.816 --> 00:31.796 align:middle
Today my colleagues on the
Federal Open Market Committee

00:31.796 --> 00:35.216 align:middle
and I made some important
changes to our policy statement,

00:35.746 --> 00:38.926 align:middle
including an update to our
guidance for the likely path

00:38.926 --> 00:40.966 align:middle
of our policy interest rate.

00:42.066 --> 00:44.406 align:middle
Guided by our new
Statement on Longer-Run Goals

00:44.406 --> 00:45.956 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy

00:45.956 --> 00:47.296 align:middle
that we announced
a few weeks ago,

00:47.986 --> 00:50.576 align:middle
these changes clarify
our strong commitment

00:50.726 --> 00:52.456 align:middle
over a longer time horizon.

00:54.376 --> 00:56.326 align:middle
Before describing
today's policy actions,

00:56.326 --> 00:59.686 align:middle
let me briefly review recent
economic developments.

01:01.716 --> 01:03.006 align:middle
Economic activity has picked

01:03.006 --> 01:05.426 align:middle
up from its depressed
second-quarter level,

01:06.036 --> 01:07.826 align:middle
when much of the
economy was shut

01:07.826 --> 01:09.646 align:middle
down to stem the
spread of the virus.

01:10.176 --> 01:13.856 align:middle
With the reopening of many
businesses and factories

01:13.856 --> 01:16.556 align:middle
and fewer people withdrawing
from social interactions,

01:16.996 --> 01:19.856 align:middle
household spending looks to have
recovered about three-quarters

01:19.856 --> 01:21.036 align:middle
of its earlier decline.

01:22.696 --> 01:25.076 align:middle
Nonetheless, spending
on services

01:25.076 --> 01:28.486 align:middle
that typically require people to
gather closely, including travel

01:28.486 --> 01:30.446 align:middle
and hospitality, is
still quite weak.

01:31.706 --> 01:34.976 align:middle
The recovery in household
spending also likely owes

01:34.976 --> 01:39.266 align:middle
to federal stimulus payments and
expanded unemployment benefits,

01:39.486 --> 01:41.336 align:middle
which provided substantial

01:41.336 --> 01:43.436 align:middle
and timely support
to household incomes.

01:45.226 --> 01:48.096 align:middle
Activity in the housing sector
has returned to its level

01:48.096 --> 01:51.366 align:middle
at the beginning of the year,
and we are starting to see signs

01:51.366 --> 01:53.146 align:middle
of an improvement in
business investment.

01:54.516 --> 01:57.676 align:middle
The recovery has progressed more
quickly than generally expected,

01:57.676 --> 01:59.886 align:middle
and forecasts from
FOMC participants

01:59.886 --> 02:02.656 align:middle
for economic growth this
year have been revised

02:02.656 --> 02:05.186 align:middle
up since our June Summary
of Economic Projections.

02:06.566 --> 02:09.856 align:middle
Even so, overall activity
remains well below its level

02:09.856 --> 02:13.296 align:middle
before the pandemic and the path
ahead remains highly uncertain.

02:13.646 --> 02:18.166 align:middle
In the labor market, roughly
half of the 22 million jobs

02:18.166 --> 02:21.046 align:middle
that were lost in March and
April have been regained

02:21.226 --> 02:22.796 align:middle
as many people returned to work.

02:24.196 --> 02:26.716 align:middle
The unemployment rate declined
over the past four months

02:26.756 --> 02:30.046 align:middle
but remains elevated at
8.4 percent as of August.

02:31.266 --> 02:33.836 align:middle
Although we welcome this
progress, we will not lose sight

02:33.836 --> 02:36.286 align:middle
of the millions of Americans
who remain out of work.

02:37.496 --> 02:40.886 align:middle
Looking ahead, FOMC participants
project the unemployment rate

02:40.886 --> 02:45.916 align:middle
to continue to decline; the
median projection is 7.6 percent

02:45.916 --> 02:49.846 align:middle
at the end of this year,
5.5 percent next year,

02:50.386 --> 02:52.636 align:middle
and 4 percent by 2023.

02:54.156 --> 02:57.816 align:middle
The economic downturn has not
fallen equally on all Americans,

02:58.246 --> 03:00.636 align:middle
and those least able to
shoulder the burden have been

03:00.636 --> 03:01.266 align:middle
hardest hit.

03:02.106 --> 03:03.506 align:middle
In particular, the high level

03:03.506 --> 03:05.586 align:middle
of joblessness has
been especially severe

03:05.586 --> 03:09.566 align:middle
for lower-wage workers in the
services sector, for women,

03:10.556 --> 03:12.666 align:middle
and for African Americans
and Hispanics.

03:12.816 --> 03:17.296 align:middle
The economic dislocation
has upended many lives

03:17.346 --> 03:19.676 align:middle
and created great
uncertainty about the future.

03:20.256 --> 03:23.916 align:middle
The pandemic has also
left a significant imprint

03:23.916 --> 03:24.556 align:middle
on inflation.

03:25.676 --> 03:29.556 align:middle
For some goods, including food,
supply constraints have led

03:29.556 --> 03:32.226 align:middle
to notably higher prices,
adding to the burden

03:32.226 --> 03:33.876 align:middle
for those struggling
with lost income.

03:34.916 --> 03:37.966 align:middle
More broadly, however, weaker
demand, especially in sectors

03:37.966 --> 03:40.036 align:middle
that have been most
affected by the pandemic,

03:40.486 --> 03:43.486 align:middle
has held down consumer
prices, and overall,

03:43.666 --> 03:46.206 align:middle
inflation is running
well below our 2 percent

03:46.206 --> 03:47.166 align:middle
longer-run objective.

03:47.586 --> 03:51.946 align:middle
The median inflation projection
from FOMC participants rises

03:51.946 --> 03:56.036 align:middle
from 1.2 percent this year
to 1.7 percent next year

03:56.596 --> 03:59.326 align:middle
and reaches 2 percent in 2023.

04:00.236 --> 04:04.356 align:middle
As the economy began its
recovery, COVID-19 cases,

04:04.356 --> 04:07.266 align:middle
hospitalizations,
and deaths also rose.

04:08.266 --> 04:10.946 align:middle
The reimposition of some
social-distancing restrictions

04:10.946 --> 04:12.566 align:middle
as well as more cautious
behavior

04:12.566 --> 04:14.616 align:middle
by many individuals
have succeeded

04:14.616 --> 04:16.486 align:middle
in slowing the spread
of the virus.

04:17.626 --> 04:19.816 align:middle
As we have emphasized
throughout the pandemic,

04:19.886 --> 04:22.756 align:middle
the outlook for the economy
is extraordinarily uncertain

04:22.756 --> 04:25.266 align:middle
and will depend in large
part on our success

04:25.266 --> 04:26.536 align:middle
in keeping the virus in check.

04:27.586 --> 04:28.966 align:middle
All of us have a role to play

04:28.966 --> 04:30.786 align:middle
in our nation's response
to the pandemic.

04:31.626 --> 04:34.146 align:middle
Following the advice of
public health professionals

04:34.766 --> 04:37.316 align:middle
to keep appropriate social
distances and to wear masks

04:37.316 --> 04:40.326 align:middle
in public will help get the
economy back to full strength.

04:40.926 --> 04:43.966 align:middle
A full economic recovery
is unlikely

04:43.966 --> 04:47.016 align:middle
until people are confident
that it is safe to reengage

04:47.016 --> 04:48.556 align:middle
in a broad range of activities.

04:49.076 --> 04:52.266 align:middle
The path forward
will also depend

04:52.266 --> 04:55.086 align:middle
on the policy actions
taken across all parts

04:55.176 --> 04:56.716 align:middle
of the government
to provide relief

04:56.716 --> 04:59.066 align:middle
and to support the recovery
for as long as needed.

05:00.496 --> 05:03.186 align:middle
The Federal Reserve's response
to this crisis has been guided

05:03.186 --> 05:05.626 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:05.896 --> 05:08.036 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people,

05:08.486 --> 05:11.016 align:middle
along with our responsibilities
to promote the stability

05:11.016 --> 05:12.066 align:middle
of the financial system.

05:12.616 --> 05:16.146 align:middle
We remain committed to using
our full range of tools

05:16.146 --> 05:18.916 align:middle
to support the economy
in this challenging time.

05:20.656 --> 05:23.976 align:middle
The changes we made in today's
policy statement reflect our

05:23.976 --> 05:27.046 align:middle
strategy to achieve our
dual-mandate goals by seeking

05:27.046 --> 05:29.446 align:middle
to eliminate shortfalls
from maximum employment

05:29.916 --> 05:32.666 align:middle
and achieve inflation that
averages 2 percent over time,

05:33.216 --> 05:35.906 align:middle
as we articulated in our
Statement on Longer-Run Goals

05:35.986 --> 05:37.426 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy.

05:38.586 --> 05:41.856 align:middle
We view maximum employment as a
broad-based and inclusive goal

05:41.856 --> 05:43.896 align:middle
and do not see a high
level of employment

05:43.896 --> 05:47.336 align:middle
as posing a policy concern
unless accompanied by signs

05:47.396 --> 05:50.496 align:middle
of unwanted increases in
inflation or the emergence

05:50.496 --> 05:53.406 align:middle
of other risks that could impede
the attainment of our goals.

05:54.796 --> 05:56.266 align:middle
And we believe that
achieving inflation

05:56.266 --> 05:59.076 align:middle
that averages 2 percent
over time helps ensure

05:59.076 --> 06:01.756 align:middle
that longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

06:01.756 --> 06:04.926 align:middle
anchored at our longer-run
2 percent objective.

06:05.596 --> 06:09.866 align:middle
In turn, well-anchored inflation
expectations enhance our ability

06:09.866 --> 06:12.266 align:middle
to meet both our employment
and inflation objectives,

06:12.416 --> 06:13.886 align:middle
particularly in the new normal

06:14.006 --> 06:15.766 align:middle
in which interest
rates are closer

06:15.766 --> 06:18.586 align:middle
to their effective lower
bound even in good times.

06:19.856 --> 06:21.816 align:middle
Hence, as we say
in our statement,

06:22.256 --> 06:24.706 align:middle
with inflation running
persistently below 2 percent,

06:25.326 --> 06:27.776 align:middle
we "will aim to achieve
inflation moderately

06:27.776 --> 06:29.496 align:middle
above 2 percent for some time

06:30.056 --> 06:32.866 align:middle
so that inflation averages
2 percent over time

06:33.386 --> 06:36.086 align:middle
and longer-term inflation
expectations remain well

06:36.086 --> 06:37.046 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent."

06:38.346 --> 06:40.686 align:middle
We expect "to maintain
an accommodative stance

06:40.686 --> 06:43.556 align:middle
of monetary policy until these
outcomes"-including maximum

06:43.556 --> 06:44.636 align:middle
employment-"are achieved."

06:45.876 --> 06:48.056 align:middle
With regard to interest
rates, we now indicate

06:48.056 --> 06:49.706 align:middle
that we expect it
will be appropriate

06:50.116 --> 06:53.216 align:middle
to maintain the current 0
to ¼ percent target range

06:53.216 --> 06:54.286 align:middle
for the federal funds rate

06:54.746 --> 06:57.926 align:middle
"until labor market conditions
have reached levels consistent

06:57.926 --> 07:00.266 align:middle
with the Committee's assessments
of maximum employment

07:00.776 --> 07:03.936 align:middle
and inflation has risen to
2 percent and is on track

07:03.936 --> 07:06.326 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time."

07:07.386 --> 07:11.286 align:middle
In addition, over coming months
we will continue to increase our

07:11.286 --> 07:12.976 align:middle
"holdings of Treasury securities

07:12.976 --> 07:14.616 align:middle
and agency mortgage-backed
securities

07:15.086 --> 07:16.556 align:middle
at least at the current pace."

07:17.536 --> 07:19.566 align:middle
These asset purchases
are intended

07:19.566 --> 07:21.546 align:middle
"to sustain smooth
market functioning

07:21.916 --> 07:24.476 align:middle
and help foster accommodative
financial conditions,

07:24.826 --> 07:26.516 align:middle
thereby supporting
the flow of credit

07:26.516 --> 07:27.766 align:middle
to households and businesses."

07:28.796 --> 07:31.626 align:middle
We believe the strong policy
guidance we are providing today

07:31.626 --> 07:34.446 align:middle
will serve the economy
well by promoting our goals

07:34.746 --> 07:37.296 align:middle
through the many possible
paths the recovery may take.

07:38.276 --> 07:40.346 align:middle
Of course, as we note
in our policy statement,

07:40.396 --> 07:43.156 align:middle
we "would be prepared to adjust
the stance of monetary policy

07:43.396 --> 07:46.706 align:middle
as appropriate if risks emerge
that could impede the attainment

07:46.706 --> 07:47.496 align:middle
of our [the Committee's] goals."

07:48.016 --> 07:51.606 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has
also been taking broad

07:51.606 --> 07:54.216 align:middle
and forceful actions to more
directly support the flow

07:54.216 --> 07:57.366 align:middle
of credit in the economy for
households, for businesses large

07:57.366 --> 08:00.236 align:middle
and small, and for state
and local governments.

08:01.226 --> 08:03.096 align:middle
Preserving the flow
of credit is essential

08:03.096 --> 08:04.896 align:middle
for mitigating the
damage to the economy

08:04.896 --> 08:06.706 align:middle
and promoting a robust recovery.

08:08.266 --> 08:11.036 align:middle
Many of our programs rely
on emergency lending powers

08:11.036 --> 08:13.066 align:middle
that require the support
of the Treasury Department

08:13.406 --> 08:16.476 align:middle
and are available only in
very unusual circumstances,

08:16.766 --> 08:18.796 align:middle
such as those we find
ourselves in today.

08:20.016 --> 08:22.966 align:middle
These programs serve as a
backstop to key credit markets

08:23.066 --> 08:24.986 align:middle
and appear to have
restored the flow of credit

08:25.046 --> 08:27.426 align:middle
from private lenders
through normal channels.

08:28.286 --> 08:30.026 align:middle
We have deployed
these lending powers

08:30.026 --> 08:33.086 align:middle
to an unprecedented extent,
enabled, in large part,

08:33.086 --> 08:36.416 align:middle
by financial backing and support
from Congress and the Treasury.

08:37.706 --> 08:40.096 align:middle
When the time comes, after
the crisis has passed,

08:40.096 --> 08:42.936 align:middle
we will put these emergency
tools back in the toolbox.

08:43.456 --> 08:46.086 align:middle
As I have emphasized before,

08:46.776 --> 08:49.156 align:middle
these are lending powers,
not spending powers.

08:49.616 --> 08:52.416 align:middle
The Fed cannot grant money
to particular beneficiaries.

08:52.896 --> 08:55.206 align:middle
We can only create
programs or facilities

08:55.206 --> 08:59.216 align:middle
with broad-based eligibility to
make loans to solvent entities

08:59.336 --> 09:01.626 align:middle
with the expectation that
the loans will be repaid.

09:02.586 --> 09:05.746 align:middle
Many borrowers are
benefiting from these programs,

09:05.746 --> 09:07.026 align:middle
as is the overall economy.

09:07.646 --> 09:10.126 align:middle
But for many others, getting
a loan that may be difficult

09:10.126 --> 09:12.146 align:middle
to repay may not be the answer.

09:12.616 --> 09:15.286 align:middle
In these cases, direct
fiscal support may be needed.

09:16.416 --> 09:18.846 align:middle
Elected officials have
the power to tax and spend

09:18.846 --> 09:21.316 align:middle
and to make decisions about
where we, as a society,

09:21.316 --> 09:23.336 align:middle
should direct our
collective resources.

09:24.296 --> 09:25.606 align:middle
The fiscal policy actions

09:25.606 --> 09:28.526 align:middle
that have been taken thus far
have made a critical difference

09:28.526 --> 09:31.936 align:middle
to families, businesses, and
communities across the country.

09:32.996 --> 09:35.816 align:middle
Even so, the current economic
downturn is the most severe

09:35.816 --> 09:36.766 align:middle
in our lifetimes.

09:37.146 --> 09:39.096 align:middle
It will take a while to
get back to the levels

09:39.096 --> 09:42.026 align:middle
of economic activity and
employment that prevailed

09:42.186 --> 09:43.406 align:middle
at the beginning of this year,

09:43.836 --> 09:46.276 align:middle
and it may take continued
support from both monetary

09:46.276 --> 09:48.856 align:middle
and fiscal policy
to achieve that.

09:50.756 --> 09:53.326 align:middle
We understand that our actions
affect communities, families,

09:53.326 --> 09:54.786 align:middle
and businesses across
the country.

09:55.526 --> 09:58.056 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

09:58.846 --> 10:01.476 align:middle
We are committed to using
our full range of tools

10:01.476 --> 10:04.446 align:middle
to support the economy and to
help assure that the recovery

10:04.446 --> 10:08.626 align:middle
from this difficult period
will be as robust as possible.

10:10.136 --> 10:13.676 align:middle
Finally, I would like to take a
moment to recognize the passing

10:13.676 --> 10:15.806 align:middle
of our friend and
colleague, Thomas Laubach.

10:16.736 --> 10:19.936 align:middle
His outstanding analysis and
advice have been indispensable

10:19.936 --> 10:23.926 align:middle
to the FOMC and have played a
key role in the policy decisions

10:23.926 --> 10:26.396 align:middle
that will define this era
of the Federal Reserve.

10:27.126 --> 10:28.866 align:middle
He will be remembered
for his intellect,

10:28.976 --> 10:32.676 align:middle
but also his kindness, his
equanimity, and his dedication

10:32.676 --> 10:35.706 align:middle
to achieving our mission on
behalf of the American people.

10:36.836 --> 10:37.446 align:middle
We will miss him.

10:38.696 --> 10:39.066 align:middle
Thank you.

10:39.226 --> 10:40.846 align:middle
I'll now be glad to
take your questions.

10:42.146 --> 10:43.036 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:43.956 --> 10:44.346 align:middle
Thank you.

10:44.346 --> 10:45.366 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

10:45.366 --> 10:46.296 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

10:46.336 --> 10:50.276 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chair Powell.

10:50.396 --> 10:53.346 align:middle
Nick Timiraos from the
Wall Street Journal.

10:53.376 --> 10:56.916 align:middle
You've been very clear about the
Committee's intention on rates,

10:56.916 --> 10:59.846 align:middle
not even thinking about
thinking about raising rates,

11:00.306 --> 11:04.536 align:middle
and today showing low rates
even as unemployment falls

11:04.536 --> 11:07.136 align:middle
to 4 percent and inflation
rises to 2 percent.

11:07.596 --> 11:09.716 align:middle
My question is about
asset purchases.

11:10.096 --> 11:14.746 align:middle
Does the guidance today apply to
the current asset purchase pace?

11:15.506 --> 11:17.696 align:middle
Are there any macroeconomic
conditions

11:17.696 --> 11:20.846 align:middle
under which you would favor
increasing the monthly pace

11:20.846 --> 11:22.796 align:middle
of Treasury and MBS purchases?

11:23.256 --> 11:26.636 align:middle
And under what conditions would
a decrease in the monthly pace

11:26.636 --> 11:28.706 align:middle
of purchases be appropriate?

11:28.776 --> 11:29.426 align:middle
Thank you.

11:30.336 --> 11:30.886 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:30.886 --> 11:31.516 align:middle
Thank you.

11:31.516 --> 11:34.556 align:middle
So we say in our postmeeting
statement that we'll continue

11:34.556 --> 11:37.166 align:middle
to increase our securities
holdings "at least

11:37.166 --> 11:38.836 align:middle
at the current pace"
over coming months

11:38.836 --> 11:40.616 align:middle
"to sustain smooth
market functioning

11:40.616 --> 11:42.646 align:middle
and help foster accommodative
financial conditions."

11:43.076 --> 11:45.626 align:middle
That latter part is an
updating of our guidance

11:45.626 --> 11:49.146 align:middle
to reflect what I've been saying
in these press conferences

11:49.146 --> 11:51.956 align:middle
for some time and what other
central banks have acknowledged,

11:51.956 --> 11:54.956 align:middle
which is that the purchases
are fostering accommodative

11:54.956 --> 11:56.026 align:middle
financial conditions as well.

11:56.546 --> 11:59.656 align:middle
That amounts to roughly $80
billion a month of Treasuries

11:59.656 --> 12:02.506 align:middle
and $40 billion net
per month for MBS.

12:02.976 --> 12:05.706 align:middle
So we do-we do think that these
purchases have been effective

12:05.706 --> 12:07.976 align:middle
in, in restoring
orderly market conditions

12:08.286 --> 12:09.976 align:middle
and have supported the flow
of credit to households

12:09.976 --> 12:10.956 align:middle
and businesses, including

12:10.956 --> 12:13.346 align:middle
by fostering more accommodative
financial conditions, which,

12:13.346 --> 12:15.186 align:middle
of course, we think
is a good thing.

12:15.876 --> 12:21.956 align:middle
So, in terms of going
forward, I would just say this:

12:21.956 --> 12:24.396 align:middle
There are various
ways and margins

12:24.396 --> 12:26.326 align:middle
that we can adjust our
tools going forward,

12:26.826 --> 12:28.696 align:middle
and we'll continue to
monitor developments.

12:28.696 --> 12:31.226 align:middle
And we're prepared to adjust
our plans as appropriate.

12:31.356 --> 12:32.076 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:32.496 --> 12:34.986 align:middle
If I could-if I could follow up,

12:35.256 --> 12:37.636 align:middle
I suppose the question I
have is, why give guidance

12:37.636 --> 12:40.236 align:middle
on one policy tool
but not give guidance

12:40.236 --> 12:42.766 align:middle
on the other policy tool
when the Fed has talked

12:42.766 --> 12:45.376 align:middle
about those two policy
tools working together?

12:46.816 --> 12:47.366 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:47.366 --> 12:50.466 align:middle
So we, we think our-we think
our policy stance is appropriate

12:50.466 --> 12:53.236 align:middle
today, and we're prepared to
adjust it going forward as we,

12:53.356 --> 12:55.006 align:middle
as we see appropriate.

12:55.556 --> 12:56.596 align:middle
And today we believe

12:56.596 --> 12:59.756 align:middle
that particularly this very
strong forward guidance-very

12:59.756 --> 13:01.226 align:middle
powerful forward guidance

13:01.226 --> 13:03.856 align:middle
that we've announced today
will provide strong support

13:03.856 --> 13:04.466 align:middle
for the economy.

13:04.466 --> 13:05.466 align:middle
Effectively, we're saying

13:06.146 --> 13:08.306 align:middle
that rates will remain
highly accommodative

13:08.306 --> 13:10.986 align:middle
until the economy is far along
in its recovery, and that,

13:10.986 --> 13:12.446 align:middle
that should be a very
powerful statement

13:12.446 --> 13:13.996 align:middle
in supporting economic activity.

13:14.526 --> 13:18.516 align:middle
Now we're buying $120 billion
in securities per month

13:19.116 --> 13:21.026 align:middle
across the-across
the Treasury curve.

13:21.026 --> 13:23.806 align:middle
That's also adding
to accommodation.

13:24.116 --> 13:26.746 align:middle
We do have the flexibility
to adjust that tool and,

13:26.746 --> 13:28.826 align:middle
and the rate tool and,
and other tools as well.

13:29.206 --> 13:31.256 align:middle
But as for right now,
we think-we think

13:31.256 --> 13:32.896 align:middle
that our policy setting
is appropriate

13:32.896 --> 13:34.686 align:middle
to support the expansion.

13:34.716 --> 13:36.036 align:middle
We did-we said from
the beginning

13:36.036 --> 13:42.096 align:middle
that we would first try
to provide some support

13:42.096 --> 13:45.556 align:middle
and stability and relief in
the first phase of the crisis,

13:45.556 --> 13:48.636 align:middle
the acute phase, and then we
would support the expansion

13:48.636 --> 13:49.246 align:middle
when it came.

13:49.246 --> 13:49.926 align:middle
Well, it's here.

13:50.576 --> 13:51.816 align:middle
And it's well along.

13:51.816 --> 13:54.176 align:middle
And so that's why we
changed our guidance today,

13:54.176 --> 13:56.026 align:middle
and we do have the
flexibility to do more

13:56.026 --> 13:56.966 align:middle
when we think it's appropriate.

13:58.256 --> 13:58.816 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:59.666 --> 14:00.486 align:middle
Thank you.

14:02.196 --> 14:02.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:02.946 --> 14:03.436 align:middle
Thank you.

14:03.436 --> 14:04.596 align:middle
Jeanna. JEANNA SMIALEK.

14:04.986 --> 14:06.536 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

14:06.536 --> 14:08.026 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

14:08.026 --> 14:10.166 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, New York Times.

14:10.266 --> 14:12.486 align:middle
I was wondering, you
beefed up your language

14:12.486 --> 14:14.806 align:middle
on financial stability
in the long-run statement

14:14.806 --> 14:19.126 align:middle
that you unveiled with your
Jackson Hole speech last month.

14:19.126 --> 14:21.946 align:middle
I'm wondering if you could kind
of walk us through how you think

14:21.946 --> 14:24.886 align:middle
about financial stability
concerns as a factor in guiding

14:24.886 --> 14:26.516 align:middle
and interpreting increases.

14:26.516 --> 14:28.786 align:middle
You know, would financial
stability concerns

14:28.786 --> 14:32.016 align:middle
on their own be enough to merit
changes in the fed funds rate?

14:32.016 --> 14:35.196 align:middle
Or would they have to come in
conjunction with an overheating

14:35.196 --> 14:37.716 align:middle
on inflation or some
sort of dramatic drop

14:37.716 --> 14:38.766 align:middle
in the unemployment rate?

14:38.766 --> 14:41.416 align:middle
If you could, sort
of, give us an outline

14:41.416 --> 14:44.176 align:middle
of your thinking there, please.

14:44.266 --> 14:44.576 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:44.576 --> 14:48.096 align:middle
So what we said in our
Statement on Longer-Run Goals

14:48.096 --> 14:50.506 align:middle
and Monetary Policy Strategy was

14:51.106 --> 14:53.606 align:middle
that the Committee's
policy decisions reflect

14:53.606 --> 14:55.656 align:middle
"its longer-run goals,
its medium-term outlook,

14:55.656 --> 14:57.366 align:middle
and its assessment[s]
of the balance of risks,

14:57.726 --> 14:59.576 align:middle
including risks to
the financial system

14:59.576 --> 15:03.006 align:middle
that could impede the attainment
of the Committee's goals."

15:03.176 --> 15:06.566 align:middle
So that's what we said
about financial stability.

15:07.006 --> 15:10.756 align:middle
And today we said that we'd "be
prepared to adjust the stance

15:10.756 --> 15:13.306 align:middle
of monetary policy as
appropriate if risks emerge

15:13.306 --> 15:15.116 align:middle
that could impede the attainment
of our [the Committee's] goals."

15:16.016 --> 15:17.446 align:middle
But you asked specifically
about,

15:17.446 --> 15:18.586 align:middle
about financial stability.

15:18.586 --> 15:20.646 align:middle
One thing I would say is

15:20.646 --> 15:24.976 align:middle
that financial-that monetary
policy should not be the first

15:24.976 --> 15:27.026 align:middle
line of defense-is not
the first line of defense

15:27.026 --> 15:29.216 align:middle
on financial stability.

15:29.276 --> 15:33.386 align:middle
We look to more appropriate
tools in the first instance

15:33.386 --> 15:36.416 align:middle
as a first line of defense,
and those would be regulation,

15:36.416 --> 15:40.346 align:middle
supervision, high capital, high
liquidity, stress testing-all

15:40.436 --> 15:43.656 align:middle
of those things,
macroprudential tools.

15:43.656 --> 15:47.196 align:middle
All of those things are, are
really the first line of defense

15:47.196 --> 15:48.006 align:middle
on financial stability.

15:48.006 --> 15:51.136 align:middle
But we always leave
open the idea

15:51.136 --> 15:55.306 align:middle
that we will not ignore those
kinds of risks and other kinds

15:55.306 --> 15:58.976 align:middle
of risks, more broadly, that
could impede the attainment

15:58.976 --> 16:02.006 align:middle
of our goals in, in
setting monetary policy.

16:02.296 --> 16:06.026 align:middle
So that's, that's really
how we think about it,

16:06.136 --> 16:08.296 align:middle
but principally that, you know,

16:08.416 --> 16:10.756 align:middle
other tools are the-are the
frontline, as I mentioned.

16:12.396 --> 16:13.296 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

16:13.296 --> 16:14.596 align:middle
Just to follow up quickly, if,

16:14.596 --> 16:16.396 align:middle
if other tools aren't
forthcoming,

16:16.396 --> 16:18.576 align:middle
would financial stability
concerns in

16:18.576 --> 16:20.756 align:middle
and of themselves be enough
to warrant a rate hike?

16:21.786 --> 16:22.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:22.646 --> 16:24.106 align:middle
So, you know, what we said again

16:24.206 --> 16:25.226 align:middle
in the [Statement
on] Longer-Run [Goals

16:25.226 --> 16:25.416 align:middle
and Monetary Policy
Strategy]-you know,

16:25.416 --> 16:27.996 align:middle
in our consensus
statement is that we,

16:28.216 --> 16:30.856 align:middle
you know-"policy
decisions reflect ...

16:31.596 --> 16:34.156 align:middle
the balance of risks, including
risks to the financial system

16:34.156 --> 16:35.896 align:middle
that could impede the attainment
of the Committee's goals."

16:35.896 --> 16:38.516 align:middle
So the test would be,
you know, does a majority

16:38.516 --> 16:39.806 align:middle
of the Committee feel that,

16:40.326 --> 16:44.696 align:middle
that monetary policy
is, is triggering that?

16:44.696 --> 16:46.546 align:middle
And that, that would
be-that would be the test.

16:46.816 --> 16:49.216 align:middle
And, you know, it's not
something that we've done.

16:49.516 --> 16:52.336 align:middle
We do monitor financial
stability concerns, of course,

16:52.876 --> 16:54.586 align:middle
intensely and regularly.

16:55.256 --> 16:58.206 align:middle
We try to use our other tools
on them, but we, we do keep them

16:58.206 --> 17:00.026 align:middle
in mind as we think
about monetary policy.

17:00.026 --> 17:00.626 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:01.566 --> 17:01.836 align:middle
Thank you.

17:02.036 --> 17:02.946 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

17:03.016 --> 17:03.526 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

17:03.526 --> 17:06.226 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I wonder

17:06.226 --> 17:10.176 align:middle
if you could help me
understand how the projections

17:10.176 --> 17:13.006 align:middle
of the Committee line up with
the goals of the Committee.

17:13.006 --> 17:18.136 align:middle
You've now altered the
projections to-the statement

17:18.136 --> 17:20.696 align:middle
to aim for inflation
above 2 percent.

17:21.096 --> 17:22.236 align:middle
But when I look at the SEP,

17:22.236 --> 17:27.656 align:middle
I don't see the Committee
believing a single year

17:27.736 --> 17:31.206 align:middle
in the next four years that
you are ever above 2 percent.

17:31.206 --> 17:34.876 align:middle
In fact, for each year, you
are below it until 2023,

17:34.876 --> 17:37.726 align:middle
which is the first time that
you actually hit 2 percent.

17:37.726 --> 17:41.086 align:middle
So do you think-are, are you
confident-is this just the

17:41.086 --> 17:43.046 align:middle
Committee is not confident

17:43.046 --> 17:45.396 align:middle
that not only can it not
hit its 2 percent goal,

17:45.706 --> 17:48.676 align:middle
but that now it can't hit its
goal of being above 2 percent?

17:49.136 --> 17:49.596 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:49.596 --> 17:50.076 align:middle
Not at all.

17:50.076 --> 17:52.516 align:middle
And, you know, you don't-you
also don't see people,

17:52.636 --> 17:53.996 align:middle
by and large, lifting off

17:54.126 --> 17:55.716 align:middle
or raising interest
rates above zero.

17:56.176 --> 17:58.096 align:middle
I guess there are 4
exceptions out of a Committee

17:58.096 --> 18:01.506 align:middle
of 17 during that-during
the forecast period.

18:01.506 --> 18:03.716 align:middle
So we don't reach 2 percent,
but we get very close

18:03.716 --> 18:04.726 align:middle
to it in the forecast.

18:05.886 --> 18:08.306 align:middle
We reach 2 percent-I guess
the median is 2 percent

18:08.306 --> 18:09.956 align:middle
at the end of 2023.

18:10.456 --> 18:12.846 align:middle
So, you know, you know
what the guidance says.

18:12.846 --> 18:15.896 align:middle
It says that we expect that
the current setting of,

18:15.896 --> 18:18.046 align:middle
of our rates will
be-what we expect [is]

18:18.046 --> 18:20.796 align:middle
that it will be appropriate
until such time

18:20.796 --> 18:24.356 align:middle
as we reach 2 percent
inflation, that we feel

18:24.356 --> 18:27.236 align:middle
that labor market conditions are
consistent with our assessment

18:27.236 --> 18:29.376 align:middle
of maximum employment,
and that we're on track

18:29.376 --> 18:31.436 align:middle
to achieve inflation
moderate-inflation

18:31.436 --> 18:32.136 align:middle
moderately above.

18:32.136 --> 18:32.806 align:middle
So that's the test.

18:32.806 --> 18:34.216 align:middle
So I don't think
there's any conflict

18:34.216 --> 18:36.446 align:middle
between those two
because, you know, the,

18:36.796 --> 18:37.686 align:middle
the way they're set up,

18:37.686 --> 18:39.976 align:middle
the projections don't
show the out years.

18:40.526 --> 18:43.696 align:middle
You asked about confidence,
and I would say that this,

18:43.846 --> 18:47.596 align:middle
this very strong, very
powerful guidance shows both our

18:47.596 --> 18:49.016 align:middle
confidence and our
determination.

18:49.136 --> 18:51.296 align:middle
It shows our confidence
that we can reach this goal

18:51.296 --> 18:52.586 align:middle
and our determination to do so.

18:53.056 --> 18:54.826 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

18:54.826 --> 18:56.196 align:middle
I'm sorry, if I could
just follow

18:56.196 --> 18:57.546 align:middle
up without being
simplistic about this.

18:57.546 --> 19:00.166 align:middle
But why wouldn't-if the
Committee was confident

19:00.496 --> 19:03.236 align:middle
that it could reach its new
goal of aiming for inflation

19:03.236 --> 19:06.286 align:middle
above 2 percent, why
wouldn't one of those years

19:06.286 --> 19:08.966 align:middle
at least show inflation
being above 2 percent

19:08.966 --> 19:11.356 align:middle
on the median forecast?

19:11.476 --> 19:11.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:11.996 --> 19:14.636 align:middle
Because it-we think,
looking at everything we know

19:14.636 --> 19:16.656 align:middle
about inflation dynamics
in the United States

19:16.656 --> 19:18.456 align:middle
and around the world
over recent decades,

19:18.846 --> 19:20.256 align:middle
we expect it will
take some time.

19:20.646 --> 19:24.276 align:middle
We expect that the economy
will recover quickly now,

19:24.336 --> 19:28.426 align:middle
but that that pace will slow
as, as people go back to work.

19:28.426 --> 19:30.876 align:middle
And we'll still have an area
of the economy-a big area

19:30.876 --> 19:32.266 align:middle
of the economy-that struggles.

19:32.486 --> 19:34.066 align:middle
There will be slack
in the economy.

19:34.066 --> 19:37.066 align:middle
The economy will be
below maximum employment,

19:37.066 --> 19:37.926 align:middle
below full demand.

19:37.926 --> 19:41.776 align:middle
And that will tend to
wear-to put downward pressure

19:41.776 --> 19:42.446 align:middle
on inflation.

19:42.756 --> 19:47.026 align:middle
So we think that once we get up
closer to maximum employment,

19:47.486 --> 19:49.686 align:middle
we think that inflation
will come back, generally.

19:49.686 --> 19:52.356 align:middle
And, I mean, that's sort of
what happened during the last

19:52.356 --> 19:53.176 align:middle
long expansion.

19:53.676 --> 19:56.246 align:middle
It's a slow process, but-but
there is a process there.

19:56.246 --> 19:57.946 align:middle
Inflation does move
up over time.

19:58.376 --> 20:01.586 align:middle
We do expect that will continue
today, and we expect that our,

20:01.586 --> 20:04.816 align:middle
our guidance is powerful
and will help that outcome.

20:04.816 --> 20:07.436 align:middle
We think that-that
effectively saying

20:07.436 --> 20:09.536 align:middle
that policy will remain
highly accommodative

20:09.536 --> 20:11.976 align:middle
until the economy
is very far along

20:11.976 --> 20:15.516 align:middle
in its recovery should provide
strong support for the economy

20:15.516 --> 20:17.066 align:middle
and get us there sooner
rather than later.

20:17.796 --> 20:18.926 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

20:18.926 --> 20:19.156 align:middle
Thank you.

20:19.156 --> 20:20.356 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:20.356 --> 20:20.976 align:middle
Thank you.

20:20.976 --> 20:21.436 align:middle
Rachel Siegel.

20:21.436 --> 20:24.036 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

20:24.036 --> 20:24.696 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

20:24.696 --> 20:26.346 align:middle
Thanks very much for
taking my question.

20:26.346 --> 20:30.616 align:middle
I wanted to ask if you
anticipate a slowing in the pace

20:30.616 --> 20:34.236 align:middle
of the recovery if there is not
another stimulus package and,

20:34.236 --> 20:36.986 align:middle
specifically, if there are
particular holes still remaining

20:36.986 --> 20:38.836 align:middle
in the economy that you
think could be helped

20:39.196 --> 20:40.276 align:middle
by more aid from Congress.

20:40.506 --> 20:41.206 align:middle
Thanks very much.

20:41.206 --> 20:41.996 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:42.606 --> 20:46.906 align:middle
Sure. So, first, if
you look at the Summary

20:46.906 --> 20:51.646 align:middle
of Economic Projections that
my colleagues and I filed

20:51.716 --> 20:54.506 align:middle
for this meeting, what
you'll see is an expectation

20:54.506 --> 20:57.866 align:middle
that the recovery will
continue-that it will continue

20:57.866 --> 21:01.596 align:middle
at a reasonable pace
through 2021, '22, and '23.

21:02.236 --> 21:04.896 align:middle
We do expect that that
pace will slow just

21:04.896 --> 21:06.536 align:middle
because you would
expect that the,

21:07.026 --> 21:10.696 align:middle
the pace would be fastest right
at the beginning of the-right

21:10.696 --> 21:12.496 align:middle
at the beginning
of the recovery,

21:12.496 --> 21:13.866 align:middle
because you had such
a sharp decline.

21:13.866 --> 21:17.486 align:middle
You would expect that the third
quarter should be the fastest

21:17.486 --> 21:19.546 align:middle
gains, and that, after
that, the pace should slow

21:19.546 --> 21:20.776 align:middle
down to a more normal pace.

21:20.776 --> 21:22.376 align:middle
So we do expect that.

21:22.936 --> 21:27.746 align:middle
In terms of fiscal policy-you
asked about fiscal policy.

21:28.216 --> 21:31.396 align:middle
So, you know, one thing-I
guess I would start by saying

21:31.396 --> 21:36.536 align:middle
that the initial response from
fiscal authorities was rapid.

21:36.536 --> 21:38.816 align:middle
It was forceful and
pretty effective.

21:39.166 --> 21:42.866 align:middle
And we're seeing the results
of that in-today in income

21:42.866 --> 21:45.496 align:middle
and household spending data,
in the labor market data,

21:45.496 --> 21:46.666 align:middle
in the construction data,

21:46.766 --> 21:49.606 align:middle
in the data for business
equipment spending, and the fact

21:49.606 --> 21:52.406 align:middle
that businesses are staying
in business, and, you know,

21:52.406 --> 21:55.006 align:middle
the pace of default and things
like that has really slowed.

21:55.646 --> 21:58.766 align:middle
So there's been a
really positive effect.

21:59.286 --> 22:02.746 align:middle
That said, my sense is that
more fiscal support is likely

22:02.746 --> 22:03.316 align:middle
to be needed.

22:03.896 --> 22:05.626 align:middle
Of course, the details
of that are

22:05.626 --> 22:07.066 align:middle
for Congress, not for the Fed.

22:07.576 --> 22:11.036 align:middle
But I would just say, there are
still roughly 11 million people

22:11.966 --> 22:14.326 align:middle
still out of work due to the
pandemic, and a good part

22:14.326 --> 22:16.316 align:middle
of those people were
working in industries

22:16.316 --> 22:17.326 align:middle
that are likely to struggle.

22:18.396 --> 22:20.346 align:middle
Those people may need
additional, additional support

22:20.346 --> 22:21.776 align:middle
as they try to find their way

22:21.946 --> 22:25.416 align:middle
through what will be a
difficult time for them.

22:25.546 --> 22:28.726 align:middle
We've also got struggling small
businesses, especially those

22:28.726 --> 22:30.906 align:middle
in the business of facing
directly to the public.

22:31.356 --> 22:33.626 align:middle
And we have state and local
governments dealing with a drop

22:33.626 --> 22:36.036 align:middle
in revenue at the same
time spending has gone up,

22:36.426 --> 22:39.116 align:middle
much of it related to the
pandemic and economic effects.

22:39.796 --> 22:40.846 align:middle
So, again, I would say,

22:40.846 --> 22:43.226 align:middle
the fiscal support
has been essential in,

22:43.226 --> 22:45.226 align:middle
in the good progress we see now.

22:45.926 --> 22:48.666 align:middle
And, finally, I'll
note that just

22:48.666 --> 22:50.576 align:middle
about all-the overwhelming
majority of,

22:50.716 --> 22:52.116 align:middle
of private forecasters who,

22:52.176 --> 22:56.436 align:middle
who project an ongoing recovery
are assuming there will be

22:56.436 --> 22:59.366 align:middle
substantial additional
fiscal support.

22:59.516 --> 22:59.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:02.976 --> 23:03.396 align:middle
Thank you.

23:03.396 --> 23:03.866 align:middle
Mike Derby.

23:03.866 --> 23:08.026 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

23:08.026 --> 23:09.806 align:middle
[Inaudible] taking my question.

23:09.806 --> 23:13.796 align:middle
Should we expect any further
evolutions in forward guidance,

23:13.796 --> 23:17.346 align:middle
say, maybe an adoption of
something akin to the Evans rule

23:17.346 --> 23:18.966 align:middle
that we had a few years ago?

23:18.966 --> 23:20.006 align:middle
Or is there something else

23:20.006 --> 23:23.406 align:middle
that the Fed might be
considering in the future?

23:23.466 --> 23:24.816 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

23:24.986 --> 23:27.136 align:middle
Well, so we think that the
forward guidance we adopted

23:27.136 --> 23:32.916 align:middle
today is appropriate and,
as I mentioned, powerful.

23:33.456 --> 23:38.516 align:middle
Effectively, what it says is
that we'll, we will keep policy

23:38.516 --> 23:41.226 align:middle
where it is now-keep the
rate policy where it is now

23:41.886 --> 23:46.636 align:middle
until unemployment reaches
the Committee's assessments

23:46.636 --> 23:48.896 align:middle
or levels that are-sorry,
not unemployment,

23:48.956 --> 23:52.276 align:middle
labor market conditions reach
levels that are consistent

23:52.406 --> 23:54.826 align:middle
with the Committee's assessments
of maximum employment,

23:55.216 --> 23:58.106 align:middle
until inflation reaches 2
percent, and until it's on track

23:58.106 --> 24:02.456 align:middle
to go above 2 percent
moderately for some time.

24:02.456 --> 24:05.316 align:middle
So that's very strong
forward guidance, and we think

24:05.316 --> 24:08.136 align:middle
that that will be
durable guidance

24:08.136 --> 24:09.856 align:middle
that will provide
significant support

24:09.856 --> 24:11.306 align:middle
to the economy in coming years.

24:11.306 --> 24:13.936 align:middle
So that's, that's
really our thinking on,

24:14.176 --> 24:15.866 align:middle
on forward guidance on rates.

24:15.946 --> 24:16.456 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:20.376 --> 24:20.756 align:middle
Thank you.

24:20.756 --> 24:21.576 align:middle
Howard Schneider.

24:21.576 --> 24:24.566 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

24:24.746 --> 24:26.646 align:middle
Thanks. Thanks, Chair
Powell, for doing this.

24:26.646 --> 24:28.646 align:middle
So to follow up on
Mike's question there,

24:28.766 --> 24:30.926 align:middle
since you described this
guidance as durable,

24:30.926 --> 24:35.256 align:middle
you-you've set up a three-part
test here for rate hikes:

24:35.256 --> 24:37.026 align:middle
levels consistent
with assessments

24:37.026 --> 24:40.646 align:middle
of maximum employment, inflation
has risen to 2 percent,

24:40.646 --> 24:41.466 align:middle
and you're on track

24:41.466 --> 24:44.166 align:middle
to moderately exceed 2
percent for some time.

24:44.166 --> 24:46.076 align:middle
Each of these have
modifiers, and I wonder

24:46.076 --> 24:48.466 align:middle
if you could explain
them a little bit more.

24:48.466 --> 24:51.716 align:middle
How do we pin down assessments
of maximum employment?

24:52.096 --> 24:54.586 align:middle
When you say that "inflation
has risen to 2 percent,"

24:54.586 --> 24:58.046 align:middle
does that mean 2 percent for
a day, a month, six months?

24:58.046 --> 25:01.626 align:middle
And when you say "on track
to moderately exceed,"

25:01.626 --> 25:03.736 align:middle
how should we define
"moderately"?

25:03.736 --> 25:08.356 align:middle
And how should we
define "for some time"?

25:08.726 --> 25:09.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

25:10.466 --> 25:16.286 align:middle
So, as you know, maximum
employment is not-is not

25:16.286 --> 25:18.816 align:middle
something that can be reduced to
a number the way inflation can.

25:18.816 --> 25:20.766 align:middle
It's a broad range of factors.

25:20.766 --> 25:24.976 align:middle
It really always has been and,
really, a substantial number

25:24.976 --> 25:27.276 align:middle
of factors that we've
indicated we would look at.

25:27.276 --> 25:28.866 align:middle
So it's broader labor
conditions,

25:29.566 --> 25:32.316 align:middle
consistent with our
Committee's assessment

25:32.366 --> 25:33.956 align:middle
of maximum employment.

25:34.136 --> 25:35.856 align:middle
So that would certainly
mean low unemployment,

25:35.856 --> 25:37.786 align:middle
it would mean high labor
force participation,

25:37.786 --> 25:40.366 align:middle
it would mean wages-it would
be a whole range of things.

25:40.366 --> 25:42.036 align:middle
And we're not looking at a rule.

25:42.036 --> 25:44.496 align:middle
We're looking at a
judgmental assessment,

25:44.976 --> 25:46.946 align:middle
which I think we'll
be very transparent

25:46.946 --> 25:48.496 align:middle
about as we-as we go forward.

25:49.256 --> 25:52.606 align:middle
In terms of inflation, you
know, this is a Committee

25:52.606 --> 25:55.296 align:middle
that is both confident

25:55.596 --> 26:01.726 align:middle
and committed to-and
determined to reach our goals.

26:02.416 --> 26:05.946 align:middle
And the idea that we would
look for the, the quickest way

26:05.946 --> 26:07.386 align:middle
out is just-it's
just not who we are.

26:07.386 --> 26:09.876 align:middle
It's not that-there's
no message of that here.

26:09.876 --> 26:13.546 align:middle
We would not be looking for one
month of 2 percent inflation;

26:13.546 --> 26:16.466 align:middle
we said return to 2-to
achieve 2 percent inflation.

26:16.466 --> 26:18.846 align:middle
Okay. So just understand
that, you know,

26:18.846 --> 26:19.986 align:middle
we're strongly committed

26:19.986 --> 26:21.826 align:middle
to achieving our goals
and the overshoot.

26:21.826 --> 26:23.646 align:middle
So that should tell
you about that.

26:24.226 --> 26:28.726 align:middle
Oh, in terms of-okay,
in terms of-so,

26:28.916 --> 26:30.496 align:middle
what does "moderate" mean?

26:30.496 --> 26:31.616 align:middle
It means not large.

26:31.616 --> 26:34.556 align:middle
It doesn't-it means not
very high above 2 percent.

26:34.556 --> 26:35.536 align:middle
It means moderate.

26:35.686 --> 26:37.536 align:middle
I think that's a fairly
well-understood word.

26:37.716 --> 26:39.446 align:middle
In terms of-in terms
of "for a time,"

26:39.446 --> 26:43.106 align:middle
what it means is not permanently
and not for a sustained period.

26:43.706 --> 26:47.076 align:middle
You know, we're, we're
resisting the urge to try

26:47.076 --> 26:50.486 align:middle
to create some sort of a
rule or a formula here.

26:50.946 --> 26:54.266 align:middle
And I think the, the public
will understand pretty well what

26:54.266 --> 26:54.666 align:middle
we want.

26:54.666 --> 26:56.596 align:middle
It's actually pretty
straightforward.

26:56.596 --> 26:57.726 align:middle
We want to achieve inflation

26:57.726 --> 27:00.156 align:middle
that averages 2 percent
over time.

27:00.696 --> 27:03.166 align:middle
And if we do that, inflation
expectations will be right

27:03.166 --> 27:05.906 align:middle
at 2 percent, and that'll help
us achieve 2 percent inflation

27:05.906 --> 27:07.946 align:middle
over time and avoid
the situation

27:08.316 --> 27:11.326 align:middle
where the central bank loses its
ability to support the economy.

27:11.326 --> 27:11.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:14.596 --> 27:15.046 align:middle
Thank you.

27:15.046 --> 27:16.826 align:middle
James Politi.

27:16.826 --> 27:17.676 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

27:17.916 --> 27:19.426 align:middle
Thanks for taking the question.

27:19.426 --> 27:22.276 align:middle
James Politi with
the Financial Times.

27:22.276 --> 27:26.756 align:middle
Do you consider today's
enhanced forward guidance

27:26.896 --> 27:33.926 align:middle
to have actual accommodation
to the-to the U.S. economy

27:33.926 --> 27:37.056 align:middle
from a monetary perspective
and sort

27:37.056 --> 27:41.086 align:middle
of deliver further
support for the economy?

27:41.176 --> 27:44.476 align:middle
Or is it just a tweak to
your existing policy stance?

27:44.976 --> 27:49.076 align:middle
And, in terms of fiscal
support, do you assume

27:49.076 --> 27:51.606 align:middle
in your own projections-not
just, you know,

27:51.606 --> 27:56.066 align:middle
private forecasters-that
additional fiscal support will

27:56.066 --> 27:56.916 align:middle
be forthcoming?

27:57.236 --> 27:59.716 align:middle
Or do you expect weaker growth

27:59.716 --> 28:03.966 align:middle
or a-or a larger
contraction rather this year

28:03.966 --> 28:08.866 align:middle
if no fiscal support
is forthcoming?

28:08.926 --> 28:09.656 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:10.066 --> 28:11.866 align:middle
So, in terms of the effects,

28:11.866 --> 28:17.886 align:middle
so I think what we've done
is-is more or less aligned

28:17.886 --> 28:21.136 align:middle
with the consensus
statement today.

28:21.136 --> 28:25.236 align:middle
So it's, it's in line with what,
what might have been expected.

28:25.236 --> 28:26.356 align:middle
As I mentioned, I think

28:26.356 --> 28:29.706 align:middle
over time it will provide
very powerful support

28:29.986 --> 28:31.756 align:middle
for this economy
as we move forward.

28:31.756 --> 28:35.436 align:middle
In a sense, it's
consistent with expectations,

28:35.436 --> 28:38.806 align:middle
so I don't-I'm not looking
for a big reaction right now.

28:38.806 --> 28:42.816 align:middle
But I think, over time,
again, guidance that we expect

28:42.816 --> 28:44.816 align:middle
to retain the current stance

28:44.886 --> 28:49.986 align:middle
until the economy is-has moved
very far toward our goals is a

28:49.986 --> 28:51.106 align:middle
strong and powerful thing.

28:51.566 --> 28:53.566 align:middle
And I think that
will be supportive

28:53.566 --> 28:55.806 align:middle
of the economy over time.

28:55.806 --> 28:58.236 align:middle
In terms of additional
fiscal support,

28:58.956 --> 29:04.896 align:middle
I guess your question is,
what would happen if-yes, so,

29:05.586 --> 29:07.616 align:middle
people have different
assessments, and,

29:07.616 --> 29:08.686 align:middle
and different participants

29:08.686 --> 29:11.146 align:middle
in the FOMC made different
assessments on their own.

29:11.536 --> 29:12.816 align:middle
I think broadly, though,

29:12.816 --> 29:15.556 align:middle
there is an expectation
among private forecasters

29:15.556 --> 29:18.076 align:middle
and among FOMC participants

29:18.076 --> 29:20.676 align:middle
that there will be some
further fiscal action.

29:21.086 --> 29:23.356 align:middle
And there does seem to be
an appetite on the part

29:23.356 --> 29:25.286 align:middle
of all the relevant
players to doing something.

29:25.636 --> 29:27.196 align:middle
The question is,
how much and when?

29:27.786 --> 29:31.156 align:middle
And so I would just say that
if-and it's very hard to say.

29:31.156 --> 29:33.846 align:middle
So, so far the economy
has proven resilient

29:33.846 --> 29:36.666 align:middle
to the-to the lapsing
of the-to the,

29:36.666 --> 29:38.826 align:middle
of the CARES Act
unemployment-enhanced

29:39.276 --> 29:40.766 align:middle
unemployment benefits.

29:41.296 --> 29:44.136 align:middle
But there's, there's
certainly a risk, though, that,

29:44.926 --> 29:48.376 align:middle
that those who are
unemployed have saved-appear

29:48.376 --> 29:50.286 align:middle
to have saved some of those
benefits, and they'll,

29:50.286 --> 29:53.896 align:middle
they'll now spend them, and
that, as the months pass, if,

29:54.016 --> 29:55.486 align:middle
if there's no follow-up on that,

29:55.486 --> 29:59.286 align:middle
if there isn't additional
support and there isn't a job

29:59.286 --> 30:01.836 align:middle
for the-some of those people who
are, are from industries where,

30:01.836 --> 30:03.706 align:middle
where it's going to be
very hard to find new work,

30:04.186 --> 30:06.686 align:middle
then you'll-that will start to
show up in economic activity.

30:06.686 --> 30:09.956 align:middle
It'll also show up in
things like evictions

30:09.956 --> 30:13.806 align:middle
and foreclosures and, and, you
know, things that will scar

30:13.806 --> 30:14.916 align:middle
and damage the economy.

30:14.916 --> 30:16.736 align:middle
So that's a downside risk.

30:17.476 --> 30:20.896 align:middle
So I, I think the real question
is, is, when and how much

30:20.896 --> 30:23.386 align:middle
and what will be the-what
will be the contents?

30:23.896 --> 30:26.766 align:middle
And, you know, no one-no one has
any certainty around that, but,

30:26.926 --> 30:29.976 align:middle
broadly speaking, if
we don't get that,

30:30.106 --> 30:32.286 align:middle
then there would
certainly be downside risks

30:32.286 --> 30:33.866 align:middle
through the-certainly through
the channel I mentioned.

30:33.946 --> 30:34.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

30:36.186 --> 30:37.096 align:middle
Thank you.

30:37.096 --> 30:37.766 align:middle
Anneken, CNN.

30:37.766 --> 30:39.276 align:middle
ANNEKEN TAPPE.

30:39.576 --> 30:41.906 align:middle
Thanks for taking my question.

30:41.906 --> 30:44.006 align:middle
Chairman Powell, given
the recent update

30:44.006 --> 30:46.556 align:middle
to the policy framework
and repeated calls for this

30:46.556 --> 30:50.036 align:middle
in the Fed Listens events, is
the Fed open to other measures

30:50.036 --> 30:51.996 align:middle
of the economy, such
as income inequality

30:51.996 --> 30:53.426 align:middle
and affordability of housing?

30:55.526 --> 30:56.136 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:57.316 --> 31:02.206 align:middle
So we-you know, we monitor
everything we think is important

31:03.006 --> 31:03.976 align:middle
in the U.S. economy.

31:04.026 --> 31:06.906 align:middle
And in that-in a broad sense,
all of it goes into thinking

31:06.906 --> 31:07.836 align:middle
about monetary policy.

31:07.836 --> 31:08.846 align:middle
You mentioned inequality.

31:09.296 --> 31:14.506 align:middle
So, you know, disparities
in, in income

31:14.506 --> 31:17.576 align:middle
and in financial well-being
by various demographic

31:17.576 --> 31:19.836 align:middle
and racial categories is
something we monitor carefully.

31:21.016 --> 31:25.266 align:middle
Inequality, which I would point
to-it's a multifaceted thing.

31:25.266 --> 31:27.796 align:middle
But I would point to the
relative stagnation of incomes

31:27.796 --> 31:29.796 align:middle
for people at the lower
end of the income spectrum

31:30.066 --> 31:31.256 align:middle
and also lower mobility.

31:31.786 --> 31:34.546 align:middle
So those are things that
hold back our economy.

31:34.726 --> 31:38.166 align:middle
They are. The thing is, we
don't really have the tools

31:38.166 --> 31:38.886 align:middle
to address those.

31:38.886 --> 31:42.146 align:middle
We, we have interest
rates and bank supervision

31:42.146 --> 31:44.926 align:middle
and financial stability
policy and things like that,

31:45.306 --> 31:49.006 align:middle
but we can't-we can't get at
those things through our tools.

31:49.006 --> 31:53.356 align:middle
When we lower the federal funds
rate, that supports the economy

31:53.356 --> 31:56.816 align:middle
across a broad range of,
of people and activities,

31:56.816 --> 31:58.136 align:middle
but we can't-we don't
have the ability

31:58.136 --> 32:00.446 align:middle
to target particular groups.

32:00.496 --> 32:02.906 align:middle
Notwithstanding that,
we, we do talk about it,

32:02.906 --> 32:05.316 align:middle
because these are important
features of our economy.

32:05.836 --> 32:09.116 align:middle
And we-you know, I, I think
those are-those distributional

32:09.116 --> 32:11.386 align:middle
issues are, are issues
that are really

32:11.386 --> 32:12.776 align:middle
for our elected officials.

32:13.066 --> 32:15.646 align:middle
And I would say, I
take them seriously

32:15.646 --> 32:16.996 align:middle
as holding back our economy.

32:16.996 --> 32:19.906 align:middle
The productive capacity
of the economy is limited

32:20.306 --> 32:22.476 align:middle
when not everyone
has the opportunity,

32:22.476 --> 32:26.326 align:middle
has the educational background
and, and the health care

32:26.326 --> 32:27.636 align:middle
and all the things that you need

32:27.936 --> 32:30.486 align:middle
to be an active participant
in our workforce.

32:30.956 --> 32:34.596 align:middle
So I think we can-if
we want to have the,

32:34.796 --> 32:37.966 align:middle
the highest potential output
and the-and the best output

32:37.966 --> 32:39.716 align:middle
for our economy, we
need that prosperity

32:39.716 --> 32:43.626 align:middle
to be very broadly
spread in the longer run.

32:43.706 --> 32:45.996 align:middle
And I-again, I would just
say, the Fed-you know,

32:45.996 --> 32:47.466 align:middle
we can talk about
those things a lot.

32:47.466 --> 32:50.606 align:middle
And in, in-when we think
about maximum employment

32:50.606 --> 32:54.656 align:middle
in particular, we do look
at individual groups.

32:54.656 --> 32:57.646 align:middle
So high unemployment in
a particular racial group

32:57.646 --> 33:00.946 align:middle
like African Americans when-you
know, we would look at that

33:00.946 --> 33:03.656 align:middle
as we think about whether we're
really at maximum employment.

33:03.656 --> 33:05.146 align:middle
We would look-we would
look at that along

33:05.146 --> 33:06.426 align:middle
with a lot of other data.

33:06.866 --> 33:09.466 align:middle
So the answer is, we do look
at all those things and,

33:10.116 --> 33:11.506 align:middle
and do what we can
with our tools.

33:11.506 --> 33:13.046 align:middle
But, ultimately,
these are issues

33:13.046 --> 33:14.346 align:middle
for elected representatives.

33:14.346 --> 33:14.946 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

33:16.636 --> 33:17.316 align:middle
Thank you.

33:17.316 --> 33:18.016 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

33:18.016 --> 33:19.126 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

33:19.126 --> 33:20.696 align:middle
Yes, thank you, Chairman
Powell, for the question.

33:20.696 --> 33:22.046 align:middle
I just wanted some clarity here.

33:22.046 --> 33:24.146 align:middle
At what point do you
think it's prudent

33:24.146 --> 33:26.416 align:middle
to shift the bond purchases

33:26.416 --> 33:29.216 align:middle
from market stability-as
you had said,

33:29.216 --> 33:31.946 align:middle
from the shorter-term
maturities to more longer-term,

33:31.946 --> 33:33.336 align:middle
more stimulus-related?

33:35.236 --> 33:35.776 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:35.906 --> 33:37.936 align:middle
So, you know, we
think our-we think

33:37.936 --> 33:41.116 align:middle
that our asset purchases are
doing both the thing-both those

33:41.116 --> 33:41.776 align:middle
things today.

33:41.776 --> 33:45.176 align:middle
We think, clearly, there's
been great progress,

33:45.176 --> 33:46.206 align:middle
in terms of market function.

33:46.206 --> 33:48.856 align:middle
If you remember, early in the
spring when the acute phase

33:48.856 --> 33:51.396 align:middle
of the pandemic hit, market
function was very low.

33:51.396 --> 33:54.096 align:middle
And it's improved rapidly
and, and in many respects is,

33:54.516 --> 33:55.786 align:middle
is in a good place now.

33:56.036 --> 33:58.166 align:middle
We also, though, think
that these asset purchases,

33:58.166 --> 34:00.226 align:middle
which total $120
billion a month-you know,

34:00.226 --> 34:02.786 align:middle
which is much larger
than, for example,

34:02.866 --> 34:06.486 align:middle
the last asset purchase program
during the Global Financial

34:06.536 --> 34:09.206 align:middle
Crisis and the recovery
therefrom-we think

34:09.206 --> 34:11.946 align:middle
that that's also providing
accommodative financial

34:11.946 --> 34:13.116 align:middle
conditions and supporting
growth.

34:13.116 --> 34:14.436 align:middle
And we think that's fine.

34:14.846 --> 34:15.946 align:middle
We're also aware that we,

34:15.946 --> 34:19.066 align:middle
we-there are ways we can
adjust that, you know,

34:19.126 --> 34:22.866 align:middle
to do various things-you know,
make it smaller, make it larger,

34:23.236 --> 34:28.076 align:middle
and also target different
sectors of the-of the curve.

34:28.636 --> 34:32.656 align:middle
And, you know, we're,
we're going to continue

34:32.656 --> 34:34.906 align:middle
to monitor developments,
and we're prepared

34:34.906 --> 34:36.576 align:middle
to adjust our plans
as appropriate.

34:36.576 --> 34:37.236 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:41.826 --> 34:42.276 align:middle
Thank you.

34:42.276 --> 34:44.776 align:middle
Victoria. Victoria Guida.

34:48.136 --> 34:50.496 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

34:50.496 --> 34:53.176 align:middle
Hi. Thanks for taking
my question.

34:53.176 --> 34:55.866 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
a couple of things.

34:55.866 --> 35:00.286 align:middle
First of all, if we don't
get a vaccine until well

35:00.286 --> 35:04.266 align:middle
into next year, what does
that mean for the economy?

35:04.266 --> 35:06.086 align:middle
And then, somewhat
related, I was wondering

35:06.086 --> 35:07.726 align:middle
if you could provide
any more detail

35:07.726 --> 35:10.336 align:middle
about the stress scenarios that
you all are going to release

35:10.336 --> 35:13.326 align:middle
for the big banks and
whether that is going

35:13.326 --> 35:16.806 align:middle
to be another full-blown stress
test, whether we're going

35:16.806 --> 35:18.776 align:middle
to publicly see those results,

35:18.776 --> 35:21.666 align:middle
and what it might
mean for bank payouts.

35:21.716 --> 35:22.876 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:22.936 --> 35:26.816 align:middle
Great. So, on the first one,

35:27.006 --> 35:29.386 align:middle
what's happening is
basically we're learning

35:29.386 --> 35:32.016 align:middle
to live with-right now,
we're learning to live

35:32.016 --> 35:33.886 align:middle
with COVID, which still spreads.

35:34.286 --> 35:36.986 align:middle
And we're learning to, to
engage in economic activity.

35:37.296 --> 35:39.076 align:middle
All of this recovery
that we've seen is

35:39.076 --> 35:43.086 align:middle
in a context where-you
know, where people are still

35:43.086 --> 35:48.316 align:middle
at risk of, of catching it, and
yet we're able to resume lots

35:48.316 --> 35:49.776 align:middle
and lots of economic activities.

35:50.286 --> 35:53.106 align:middle
And that involves,
as I mentioned,

35:53.106 --> 35:56.706 align:middle
you know-I think the more social
distancing we can preserve

35:56.706 --> 35:59.856 align:middle
as we go back into the
workforce-wearing masks,

35:59.856 --> 36:01.006 align:middle
keeping our distance, that kind

36:01.006 --> 36:03.166 align:middle
of thing-the better
we'll be able

36:03.166 --> 36:07.296 align:middle
to get economic activity back
up close to where it was.

36:07.596 --> 36:10.306 align:middle
I do think, though, there
are areas of the economy

36:10.306 --> 36:11.656 align:middle
that are just going
to really struggle

36:12.126 --> 36:18.336 align:middle
until we have an-a vaccine
that's, that's in wide-you know,

36:18.336 --> 36:20.226 align:middle
wide usage and is,
is widely trusted.

36:20.226 --> 36:20.916 align:middle
And those are the ones

36:20.916 --> 36:23.036 align:middle
where people were getting
really closely-close together.

36:23.276 --> 36:26.486 align:middle
I also think testing-to
the extent you have cheap

36:26.486 --> 36:30.976 align:middle
and rapid testing, you can do a
lot with that in the workforce.

36:30.976 --> 36:33.786 align:middle
You can-you can build
confidence in the workforce

36:33.786 --> 36:35.746 align:middle
if you have regular-very
regular testing

36:35.746 --> 36:36.826 align:middle
that doesn't cost very much

36:36.826 --> 36:38.236 align:middle
and you get the results
really quickly.

36:38.656 --> 36:41.406 align:middle
If you do that, you'll be able
to open a lot of workforces,

36:41.926 --> 36:45.916 align:middle
particularly in cities where
the overall case numbers are

36:45.916 --> 36:46.696 align:middle
quite low.

36:46.996 --> 36:48.566 align:middle
And that will help a lot.

36:48.566 --> 36:50.986 align:middle
So I think we're, we're going to
be finding lots and lots of ways

36:50.986 --> 36:54.236 align:middle
to get out towards-you
know, as far as we can.

36:54.236 --> 36:56.896 align:middle
There's always going to be
that-for, for some time,

36:57.346 --> 36:59.396 align:middle
there's going to be
certain activities

36:59.396 --> 37:02.216 align:middle
that will be-that will
be hard to, to resume.

37:02.676 --> 37:05.116 align:middle
So I, I think that's the
only way I can say it.

37:05.116 --> 37:07.836 align:middle
And I think trying to-you know,
we all-when we make a forecast,

37:07.836 --> 37:11.086 align:middle
we make assessments about that,
but it's really hard to say.

37:11.086 --> 37:12.646 align:middle
There is no template here.

37:12.646 --> 37:16.746 align:middle
There's no-you know, there's
no experience with this.

37:16.746 --> 37:20.116 align:middle
So, frankly, for the
last 60 days or so,

37:20.116 --> 37:22.436 align:middle
the economy's recovered
faster than expected.

37:23.066 --> 37:24.956 align:middle
And that may continue or not.

37:25.036 --> 37:25.946 align:middle
We just don't know.

37:25.946 --> 37:28.986 align:middle
And I think we should do
those things that we control

37:29.596 --> 37:32.456 align:middle
to make sure that we can
recover as quickly as possible.

37:32.456 --> 37:35.126 align:middle
And the main thing,
again, is wearing a mask

37:35.126 --> 37:36.986 align:middle
and keeping your distance
while you're in the workforce.

37:36.986 --> 37:40.636 align:middle
That's something we can all
do that will limit the spread

37:40.636 --> 37:42.696 align:middle
and let people go back to work,

37:42.966 --> 37:45.356 align:middle
avoid major outbreaks,
and things like that.

37:46.196 --> 37:49.876 align:middle
In, in terms of the
stress tests,

37:50.036 --> 37:51.316 align:middle
so I really don't
have any-you know,

37:51.316 --> 37:55.536 align:middle
we're getting ready quite soon
to be making announcements

37:55.536 --> 37:56.626 align:middle
and saying things publicly.

37:56.626 --> 38:00.146 align:middle
There's not much I can say
with you-nothing, really,

38:00.146 --> 38:01.606 align:middle
that I can say that's-on
that today.

38:01.606 --> 38:02.676 align:middle
I don't have anything for you.

38:02.676 --> 38:03.326 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:05.856 --> 38:06.636 align:middle
Thank you.

38:06.946 --> 38:07.806 align:middle
Chris Condon.

38:07.806 --> 38:09.126 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

38:09.126 --> 38:09.966 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

38:09.966 --> 38:12.576 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chairman Powell.

38:13.266 --> 38:16.576 align:middle
You have emphasized many
times, including today,

38:16.576 --> 38:20.256 align:middle
that the Fed can only
lend and not spend,

38:20.256 --> 38:22.716 align:middle
and sometimes the latter
is what's really needed.

38:22.766 --> 38:28.336 align:middle
But to the extent that a
$600 billion lending program

38:28.336 --> 38:32.076 align:middle
for small and midsize
companies could help,

38:32.076 --> 38:37.046 align:middle
what exactly is wrong with
the design or function

38:37.046 --> 38:39.156 align:middle
of the Main Street
Lending Program,

38:39.156 --> 38:41.026 align:middle
which has purchased
just, I think,

38:41.026 --> 38:43.676 align:middle
$1.4 billion in loans so far?

38:43.676 --> 38:46.736 align:middle
Eric Rosengren at the
Boston Fed has said recently

38:46.736 --> 38:51.846 align:middle
that Congress should clarify how
much risk it wants the program

38:51.956 --> 38:52.426 align:middle
to take.

38:52.426 --> 38:55.686 align:middle
But Congress has already
appropriated substantial funds

38:55.756 --> 38:58.866 align:middle
for the 13(3) programs,
and these are funds

38:58.866 --> 39:02.506 align:middle
that are explicitly
designed to absorb losses.

39:02.746 --> 39:03.756 align:middle
Meanwhile, my colleagues

39:03.756 --> 39:06.046 align:middle
who cover the banking sector
say they're being told

39:06.376 --> 39:09.706 align:middle
by commercial banks that the
Treasury Department is advising

39:09.706 --> 39:13.626 align:middle
them to target zero
losses-zero losses

39:14.116 --> 39:15.596 align:middle
in Main Street Program loans.

39:15.806 --> 39:21.986 align:middle
So, if I may, why is it that the
Federal Reserve, the Congress,

39:21.986 --> 39:27.016 align:middle
and the Treasury apparently
cannot agree on a loss tolerance

39:27.016 --> 39:31.066 align:middle
that should be applied to the
Main Street Lending Program

39:31.066 --> 39:33.176 align:middle
in a way that would
allow badly needed credit

39:33.176 --> 39:34.126 align:middle
to reach these companies?

39:34.256 --> 39:34.716 align:middle
Thank you.

39:35.096 --> 39:35.326 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

39:35.376 --> 39:38.866 align:middle
Sure. So, a couple
things about Main Street.

39:38.866 --> 39:40.586 align:middle
It, it reaches the whole nation.

39:40.846 --> 39:44.296 align:middle
It's got more than half of the
banking industry assets signed

39:44.296 --> 39:47.846 align:middle
up among the banks that are part
of it, and it's making loans.

39:47.846 --> 39:50.396 align:middle
The number is more like-it's
close to $2 billion now.

39:50.396 --> 39:52.156 align:middle
So the numbers are going up.

39:52.736 --> 39:57.526 align:middle
Banks are joining;
borrowers are coming.

39:57.876 --> 40:00.166 align:middle
And it's significant.

40:00.236 --> 40:04.326 align:middle
It's, it's relatively small now,
but it can scale up in response

40:04.326 --> 40:05.506 align:middle
to economic conditions,

40:05.506 --> 40:07.176 align:middle
should that be-should
that be appropriate.

40:07.176 --> 40:13.526 align:middle
You know, if you look
out in the lending world,

40:13.656 --> 40:14.996 align:middle
surveys generally find that,

40:14.996 --> 40:18.126 align:middle
that firms are not citing credit
constraints as a top problem.

40:18.126 --> 40:19.586 align:middle
And that, that is a lot of PPP,

40:19.586 --> 40:21.986 align:middle
bank credit lines,
and syndicated loans.

40:21.986 --> 40:27.106 align:middle
There's a lot of credit
being let out there.

40:27.376 --> 40:29.786 align:middle
So-but you're right.

40:29.956 --> 40:32.326 align:middle
We, we are looking
at some things.

40:32.326 --> 40:34.926 align:middle
We're looking at-some
lenders are concerned

40:34.926 --> 40:36.456 align:middle
about the underwriting
expectations.

40:36.946 --> 40:41.276 align:middle
So banks are going to-their
approach is likely to be

40:41.276 --> 40:43.376 align:middle
that they're going to underwrite
this loan roughly the same

40:43.376 --> 40:44.886 align:middle
as they underwrite any loan.

40:45.206 --> 40:49.916 align:middle
They're keeping part of it, and,
you know, what, what we want

40:49.916 --> 40:54.066 align:middle
to do is make sure
that-that they know

40:54.066 --> 40:56.316 align:middle
that they should take
the payment deferrals

40:56.316 --> 40:59.496 align:middle
and other things in, in place,
and also that-you know, it's,

40:59.546 --> 41:04.496 align:middle
it's really-it's really a, a
facility for, for companies

41:04.496 --> 41:07.846 align:middle
or borrowers that, that
don't have access to,

41:07.956 --> 41:09.966 align:middle
to "regular way" borrowing now.

41:10.046 --> 41:11.546 align:middle
Otherwise, why would
we need Main Street?

41:12.186 --> 41:15.826 align:middle
So that's what we're working on.

41:15.926 --> 41:17.226 align:middle
And we'll be doing some,

41:17.276 --> 41:20.416 align:middle
some-we'll be making some
changes in that respect.

41:20.966 --> 41:23.336 align:middle
I don't-I saw what
President Rosengren said.

41:23.336 --> 41:25.616 align:middle
I, I can't really
comment directly on that.

41:25.616 --> 41:30.826 align:middle
I just would say that,
you know, this is 13(3).

41:31.396 --> 41:34.986 align:middle
If you look at the law under
section 13(3), it's very clear

41:35.566 --> 41:38.176 align:middle
that we are to make loans
only to solvent borrowers.

41:38.176 --> 41:41.146 align:middle
And, and the CARES Act is
quite specific in keeping all

41:41.146 --> 41:44.806 align:middle
of the terms of, of
section 13(3) in effect,

41:44.806 --> 41:47.406 align:middle
including the requirement
that we, you know,

41:47.406 --> 41:49.716 align:middle
gather good evidence that
the borrower is solvent.

41:50.176 --> 41:53.086 align:middle
This was-this law was
amended in, you know,

41:53.086 --> 41:56.746 align:middle
under-in Dodd-Frank,
and the idea was,

41:56.936 --> 41:59.066 align:middle
was to make it challenging
and put hurdles

41:59.066 --> 42:01.806 align:middle
in place before we
made loans-at the time,

42:01.806 --> 42:03.136 align:middle
the thinking was to banks.

42:03.906 --> 42:06.406 align:middle
So now we-now we're
using that same law for,

42:06.546 --> 42:09.706 align:middle
for smaller business
borrowers, and, you know,

42:09.706 --> 42:12.286 align:middle
it doesn't-it's not
a perfect fit.

42:12.286 --> 42:16.016 align:middle
And, and, I would also just
say, for many borrowers,

42:16.616 --> 42:18.656 align:middle
they're in a situation

42:18.656 --> 42:21.886 align:middle
where their business is
still relatively shut down,

42:22.556 --> 42:24.606 align:middle
and they won't be able
to service a loan,

42:24.666 --> 42:26.996 align:middle
and so they may need
more fiscal support.

42:27.626 --> 42:30.966 align:middle
Having said that, we're,
we're continuing to work to,

42:30.966 --> 42:35.356 align:middle
to improve Main Street, to make
it more broadly available-make

42:35.526 --> 42:40.156 align:middle
it pretty much to any
company that needs it

42:40.156 --> 42:41.206 align:middle
and that can service a loan.

42:41.206 --> 42:42.656 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

42:42.656 --> 42:44.626 align:middle
And can you just very
briefly address the reports

42:44.626 --> 42:47.956 align:middle
that the Treasury is advising
banks to target zero losses?

42:48.326 --> 42:49.146 align:middle
Is that appropriate?

42:49.146 --> 42:49.986 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

42:49.986 --> 42:50.766 align:middle
I can't say.

42:50.806 --> 42:52.566 align:middle
I don't-I don't know about that.

42:52.566 --> 42:53.946 align:middle
I haven't heard those reports.

42:54.046 --> 42:59.576 align:middle
You know, again, if you think
about it, we, we weren't-we're,

42:59.576 --> 43:00.556 align:middle
we're going to have to go

43:00.556 --> 43:01.906 align:middle
through the banking
system to do this.

43:01.906 --> 43:03.506 align:middle
We-we're not going to
have a hundred thousand

43:03.506 --> 43:06.056 align:middle
or a million loan
officers working

43:06.056 --> 43:07.096 align:middle
for the Fed and the Treasury.

43:07.096 --> 43:08.686 align:middle
So we're going to go
through the banking system,

43:09.166 --> 43:11.066 align:middle
and the banks-banks
like to make good loans.

43:11.066 --> 43:11.756 align:middle
That's what they do.

43:11.756 --> 43:13.226 align:middle
They're trained to
make good loans.

43:13.736 --> 43:15.586 align:middle
So you should expect
that they-and we expect

43:15.586 --> 43:16.956 align:middle
that they will do
some underwriting.

43:17.376 --> 43:19.646 align:middle
We also want them to take
some risk, obviously,

43:19.646 --> 43:20.846 align:middle
because that was
the point of it.

43:20.846 --> 43:22.836 align:middle
And the question is,
how do you dial that in?

43:22.836 --> 43:24.916 align:middle
It's, it's not an
easy thing to do.

43:24.916 --> 43:27.466 align:middle
And, you know, we're
getting some loans made,

43:27.466 --> 43:30.016 align:middle
and we're hopeful that we'll,
that we'll clarify this

43:30.016 --> 43:32.736 align:middle
and that credit will
continue to flow.

43:32.736 --> 43:33.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

43:36.246 --> 43:36.746 align:middle
Thank you.

43:36.746 --> 43:37.726 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

43:37.726 --> 43:40.016 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

43:40.016 --> 43:43.226 align:middle
Hi, thanks for taking
my question.

43:43.226 --> 43:46.976 align:middle
Chair Powell, you've talked
a couple of times about parts

43:46.976 --> 43:48.986 align:middle
of the economy that
may not recover

43:48.986 --> 43:50.846 align:middle
as fast as we've seen so far.

43:51.236 --> 43:54.346 align:middle
Presumably you're referring
to airlines, hotels,

43:54.346 --> 43:58.196 align:middle
other sections-you know,
parts of the economy

43:58.296 --> 43:59.956 align:middle
that rely on close contact.

43:59.956 --> 44:03.566 align:middle
How are you thinking about that,
in terms of its overall impact?

44:03.566 --> 44:05.846 align:middle
Is that sector large enough

44:06.006 --> 44:09.386 align:middle
to say keep unemployment
above-you know,

44:09.386 --> 44:11.976 align:middle
far above your maximum goals?

44:12.196 --> 44:15.396 align:middle
Are you expecting that to
come back with a vaccine?

44:15.396 --> 44:18.236 align:middle
Or are a lot of those folks
going to have to find, you know,

44:18.236 --> 44:19.996 align:middle
new jobs in new industries?

44:19.996 --> 44:22.786 align:middle
And should we expect the
Fed will keep rates at zero

44:22.786 --> 44:24.446 align:middle
until all of that
reallocation is done?

44:24.446 --> 44:25.276 align:middle
Thank you.

44:25.906 --> 44:26.286 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:26.376 --> 44:29.036 align:middle
Yes, we-of course, we can't
be-we can't be really sure we

44:29.036 --> 44:30.446 align:middle
know the answers
to those questions.

44:30.846 --> 44:34.096 align:middle
But I would say, the,
the likely path is

44:34.096 --> 44:38.016 align:middle
that the-that the
expansion will continue.

44:38.406 --> 44:40.076 align:middle
And it's, as I said,
it's well along,

44:40.656 --> 44:42.836 align:middle
and it'll move most
easily through the parts

44:42.836 --> 44:46.106 align:middle
of the economy-it'll still
take some time, but the parts

44:46.106 --> 44:48.436 align:middle
of the economy that weren't
exactly directly affected,

44:48.436 --> 44:51.926 align:middle
that didn't involve getting
people in large groups together

44:51.966 --> 44:54.936 align:middle
to feed them, to fly them
around, to put them in hotels,

44:54.936 --> 44:56.636 align:middle
do entertainment, things
like that-those are going

44:56.636 --> 44:59.596 align:middle
to be the places that
are-that are very challenging.

44:59.826 --> 45:04.016 align:middle
So there will also
be the-you know,

45:04.016 --> 45:05.616 align:middle
the places that are
affected that way.

45:05.616 --> 45:08.126 align:middle
And that's going to be
challenging for, for some time.

45:08.126 --> 45:08.816 align:middle
It just is.

45:08.866 --> 45:10.546 align:middle
And we don't really know
how long that will be.

45:10.876 --> 45:12.746 align:middle
It's-you know, it's
millions of people.

45:12.746 --> 45:16.826 align:middle
As I mentioned, we had
11 million-something

45:16.826 --> 45:20.036 align:middle
like 11 million people in the
payroll survey have gone back

45:20.036 --> 45:21.596 align:middle
to work out of 22 million

45:22.126 --> 45:25.946 align:middle
who went-who lost their
jobs in March and April.

45:25.946 --> 45:26.826 align:middle
So that's half of them.

45:26.826 --> 45:30.066 align:middle
So, 11 million-particularly
if the pace of, of returning

45:30.066 --> 45:31.346 align:middle
to work slows down, it's going

45:31.346 --> 45:33.826 align:middle
to leave a large-a
large group of people.

45:33.976 --> 45:37.096 align:middle
And it'll be very meaningful
from a macroeconomic standpoint.

45:37.366 --> 45:39.926 align:middle
And our commitment is not
to forget those people.

45:39.986 --> 45:42.906 align:middle
As I mentioned, we
want-you know,

45:42.906 --> 45:44.466 align:middle
the sense of our
forward guidance is

45:44.466 --> 45:47.896 align:middle
that policy will remain, as
we've said, highly accommodative

45:47.896 --> 45:53.446 align:middle
until the-until the expansion
is well along-really,

45:53.446 --> 45:54.666 align:middle
very close to our goals.

45:54.666 --> 45:56.706 align:middle
And even after, if
we do lift off,

45:57.356 --> 45:59.056 align:middle
we will keep policy
accommodative

45:59.056 --> 46:03.016 align:middle
until we actually have
a moderate overshoot

46:03.016 --> 46:04.596 align:middle
of inflation for some time.

46:04.866 --> 46:07.626 align:middle
So those are powerful
commitments that we think will,

46:07.746 --> 46:09.866 align:middle
will support the full recovery,

46:09.866 --> 46:12.256 align:middle
including those people,
as long as it takes.

46:12.566 --> 46:12.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

46:17.546 --> 46:17.936 align:middle
Thank you.

46:17.936 --> 46:18.296 align:middle
Mike McKee.

46:18.696 --> 46:19.526 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

46:19.526 --> 46:24.346 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Michael McKee from
Bloomberg Radio and Television.

46:24.346 --> 46:26.046 align:middle
Based on what you
were just saying

46:26.046 --> 46:27.896 align:middle
about keeping policy
accommodative

46:27.896 --> 46:33.126 align:middle
for a very long time into the
recovery, lower-for-longer

46:33.276 --> 46:36.456 align:middle
as far out as three years
in your latest projections,

46:36.656 --> 46:39.396 align:middle
is that basically
it for the Fed?

46:39.396 --> 46:41.866 align:middle
In other words, since interest
rates are your main tool,

46:42.266 --> 46:45.966 align:middle
the things you can do would
push down on interest rates.

46:46.316 --> 46:48.306 align:middle
But is it really-is
it the case now

46:48.306 --> 46:51.356 align:middle
that the only additional
stimulus that can come

46:51.356 --> 46:54.106 align:middle
to the economy is
from the fiscal side?

46:55.836 --> 46:56.456 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:56.586 --> 47:00.846 align:middle
Well, no, I wouldn't-I
certainly would not say

47:00.956 --> 47:02.986 align:middle
that we're out of ammo.

47:03.486 --> 47:04.696 align:middle
Not at all.

47:05.556 --> 47:11.236 align:middle
So, first of all, we, we
do have lots of tools.

47:11.236 --> 47:13.526 align:middle
We've got the lending tools,
we've got the balance sheet,

47:14.126 --> 47:16.226 align:middle
and we've got further
forward guidance-further

47:16.226 --> 47:16.936 align:middle
forward guidance.

47:16.936 --> 47:19.926 align:middle
So we, we-there's still
plenty more that we can do.

47:19.926 --> 47:21.316 align:middle
We do think that our-that our,

47:21.816 --> 47:24.396 align:middle
our rate policy stance
is an appropriate one

47:24.396 --> 47:25.456 align:middle
to support the economy.

47:25.456 --> 47:26.446 align:middle
We think it's powerful.

47:26.966 --> 47:31.056 align:middle
And, as I mentioned, you know,
this is the kind of guidance

47:31.056 --> 47:33.366 align:middle
that will provide support
for the economy over time,

47:33.806 --> 47:37.356 align:middle
the idea being that policy will
remain highly accommodative

47:37.486 --> 47:40.596 align:middle
until the recovery
is well along-really,

47:40.596 --> 47:43.726 align:middle
very close to our goals-and then
will remain accommodative even

47:43.726 --> 47:44.526 align:middle
after we lift off.

47:44.526 --> 47:48.096 align:middle
So I think that's, that's
a really strong place for,

47:48.526 --> 47:49.936 align:middle
for rate policy to be.

47:49.986 --> 47:52.396 align:middle
But, again, we have the other
margins that we can still use.

47:52.396 --> 47:55.056 align:middle
So, no. Certainly,
we're not out of ammo.

47:55.946 --> 47:56.426 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

47:56.426 --> 47:58.786 align:middle
Well, if I could follow up,
in terms of the balance sheet,

47:59.036 --> 48:03.086 align:middle
are you concerned that your
actions are more likely

48:03.086 --> 48:05.626 align:middle
to produce asset price inflation

48:05.966 --> 48:08.466 align:middle
than goods-and-services
inflation?

48:08.466 --> 48:12.026 align:middle
In other words, are you
risking a bubble on Wall Street?

48:12.976 --> 48:13.336 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

48:13.336 --> 48:15.386 align:middle
You know, so, of course we
monitor financial conditions

48:15.386 --> 48:16.276 align:middle
very carefully.

48:16.746 --> 48:19.486 align:middle
These are-these are
not new questions.

48:19.486 --> 48:21.416 align:middle
These were questions
that were very much

48:21.416 --> 48:24.706 align:middle
in the air a decade
ago and more when,

48:24.866 --> 48:26.576 align:middle
when the Fed first
started doing QE.

48:26.576 --> 48:29.966 align:middle
And, I would say, if you look
at the long experience of,

48:30.226 --> 48:33.016 align:middle
you know, the 10-year,
8-month expansion,

48:33.016 --> 48:34.726 align:middle
the longest in our
recorded history,

48:35.506 --> 48:37.696 align:middle
it included an awful lot
of quantitative easing

48:37.696 --> 48:39.416 align:middle
and low rates for 7 years.

48:39.906 --> 48:42.446 align:middle
And, I would say, it
was notable for the lack

48:42.486 --> 48:45.826 align:middle
of the emergence
of, of some sort

48:45.826 --> 48:49.906 align:middle
of a financial bubble-a housing
bubble or some kind of a bubble,

48:50.756 --> 48:53.326 align:middle
the popping of which could
threaten the expansion.

48:53.566 --> 48:54.366 align:middle
That didn't happen.

48:54.686 --> 48:56.266 align:middle
And, frankly, it
hasn't really happened

48:56.266 --> 48:57.326 align:middle
around the world since then.

48:57.546 --> 49:00.186 align:middle
That doesn't mean that it won't
happen, but-and so, of course,

49:00.186 --> 49:01.726 align:middle
it's something that
we monitor carefully.

49:02.156 --> 49:05.376 align:middle
After the financial crisis, we
started a new-a whole division

49:05.376 --> 49:07.316 align:middle
of the Fed to focus on
financial stability.

49:07.826 --> 49:11.036 align:middle
We look at it in
every-from every perspective.

49:11.036 --> 49:13.586 align:middle
The FOMC gets briefed
on a quarterly basis.

49:14.136 --> 49:16.206 align:middle
At the Board here, we
talk about it more or less

49:16.206 --> 49:18.956 align:middle
on an ongoing basis, so it
is something we monitor.

49:18.956 --> 49:22.356 align:middle
But I don't know that
the-that the connection

49:22.356 --> 49:24.506 align:middle
between asset purchases and,

49:25.006 --> 49:27.386 align:middle
and financial stability is
a particularly tight one.

49:27.786 --> 49:34.846 align:middle
So-but again, we won't be-we
won't be just assuming that.

49:34.846 --> 49:37.356 align:middle
We'll be checking
carefully as we go.

49:37.486 --> 49:40.356 align:middle
And, by the way, the kinds
of tools that we would use

49:40.856 --> 49:43.506 align:middle
to address those sorts of things
are not really monetary policy.

49:43.506 --> 49:45.976 align:middle
It would be more tools that
strengthen the financial system.

49:45.976 --> 49:46.646 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:49.426 --> 49:49.866 align:middle
Thank you.

49:49.866 --> 49:50.876 align:middle
Don Lee. DON LEE.

49:52.396 --> 49:54.966 align:middle
Chair Powell, I'd like to ask
you about the labor market.

49:54.966 --> 49:57.346 align:middle
As you know, in August
there were

49:57.346 --> 50:03.546 align:middle
about 30 million persons
claiming unemployment benefits.

50:03.546 --> 50:07.526 align:middle
Yet the BLS jobs
report for August showed

50:07.526 --> 50:12.206 align:middle
about 13½ million unemployed,
only about 6 million more

50:12.206 --> 50:13.876 align:middle
than before the pandemic.

50:13.876 --> 50:15.456 align:middle
I wonder how you reconcile that

50:15.456 --> 50:20.016 align:middle
and what you think the actual
labor market conditions are.

50:21.216 --> 50:21.436 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:22.016 --> 50:25.336 align:middle
So, I mean, I think the
overall picture-take a step back

50:25.336 --> 50:25.676 align:middle
from this.

50:25.676 --> 50:27.176 align:middle
The overall picture is clear,

50:27.176 --> 50:30.676 align:middle
and that is that the labor
market has been recovering,

50:30.676 --> 50:33.846 align:middle
but that it's a long way-a long
way from maximum employment.

50:33.846 --> 50:37.396 align:middle
I think that's, that's
the bottom line on it.

50:37.396 --> 50:41.096 align:middle
So, within that, though-take
claims in particular.

50:41.616 --> 50:45.326 align:middle
The number of claims, the
quantity of claims, and,

50:45.326 --> 50:49.286 align:middle
frankly, the fact that PUA
claims are new-the Pandemic

50:49.286 --> 50:51.356 align:middle
Unemployment Assistance
claims-that's a new system

50:51.356 --> 50:53.176 align:middle
that had to be set up.

50:53.226 --> 50:59.086 align:middle
The actual counting of the
claims is, is volatile, and,

50:59.086 --> 51:02.906 align:middle
and it's very difficult
to take much signal

51:02.906 --> 51:04.196 align:middle
about the particular level.

51:04.196 --> 51:06.956 align:middle
So, you know, because people
were setting those systems up,

51:06.956 --> 51:09.166 align:middle
and when they got them set
up, they counted them all

51:09.166 --> 51:10.266 align:middle
at once and things like that.

51:10.706 --> 51:12.136 align:middle
I think, though,
what you've seen is

51:12.136 --> 51:13.966 align:middle
that the level of-certainly
the level

51:13.966 --> 51:17.256 align:middle
of initial claims has
declined very sharply

51:17.316 --> 51:20.676 align:middle
from the very high levels of
March and April and is now

51:20.796 --> 51:23.256 align:middle
at a lower level-continues
either

51:23.256 --> 51:25.106 align:middle
to be flat or gradually decline.

51:25.476 --> 51:28.006 align:middle
It's worth noting-and that's
good-it's worth noting

51:28.006 --> 51:32.396 align:middle
that that level is maybe
five times the level

51:32.396 --> 51:33.376 align:middle
of what claims were.

51:33.376 --> 51:38.446 align:middle
Claims were around 200,000; now
they're 900,000, in that range,

51:39.206 --> 51:41.216 align:middle
weekly for, for initial claims.

51:41.216 --> 51:44.036 align:middle
So that just tells you, the
labor market has improved,

51:44.036 --> 51:46.986 align:middle
but it's a long, long way
from maximum employment,

51:46.986 --> 51:48.626 align:middle
and it will be some
time getting back there.

51:48.996 --> 51:50.936 align:middle
I think that's the best
way to think about it.

51:51.316 --> 51:53.946 align:middle
In many parts of the
economy there's just a lot

51:53.946 --> 51:57.066 align:middle
of disruption, and
it's, it's really hard

51:57.066 --> 51:59.566 align:middle
to say precisely where we are.

51:59.986 --> 52:01.576 align:middle
I'll give you another
example with-you know,

52:01.576 --> 52:03.766 align:middle
we say unemployment's
8.4 percent.

52:03.766 --> 52:08.626 align:middle
But if you count those who
are-who are misidentified as,

52:08.706 --> 52:12.166 align:middle
as employed when they're
actually unemployed

52:12.166 --> 52:15.396 align:middle
and you add back some part of
the participation number-so

52:15.696 --> 52:17.966 align:middle
if you were-if you had a job
and you were in the labor force

52:17.966 --> 52:20.216 align:middle
in February and you lost
it because of the pandemic,

52:21.186 --> 52:22.596 align:middle
some of you are now
being reported

52:22.596 --> 52:23.596 align:middle
as out of the labor force.

52:23.596 --> 52:25.306 align:middle
But I-you know, I
would-I would more look

52:25.306 --> 52:26.336 align:middle
at those people as unemployed.

52:26.336 --> 52:28.736 align:middle
If you add those back, the level

52:28.736 --> 52:30.706 align:middle
of unemployment's
probably 3 percent higher.

52:30.936 --> 52:34.126 align:middle
On the other hand,
by that metric, the,

52:34.126 --> 52:35.836 align:middle
the unemployment
rate would have been

52:35.836 --> 52:38.456 align:middle
in the-in the 20s in, in April.

52:38.456 --> 52:40.696 align:middle
So the improvement has
been quite substantial

52:41.086 --> 52:42.076 align:middle
under any measurement.

52:42.076 --> 52:43.326 align:middle
But the level is
still quite high.

52:43.866 --> 52:47.426 align:middle
DON LEE. Well, if I could follow
up, is-is it the Fed's aim

52:47.426 --> 52:49.986 align:middle
to get back to 3.5
percent or even lower?

52:51.166 --> 52:51.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

52:51.506 --> 52:53.396 align:middle
Yes, absolutely.

52:54.126 --> 52:56.696 align:middle
You know, I, I can't be precise
about a particular number,

52:57.226 --> 52:58.836 align:middle
but let me just say,
there was a lot to like

52:58.836 --> 53:00.356 align:middle
about 3½ percent unemployment.

53:00.356 --> 53:01.396 align:middle
It's not a magic number.

53:01.396 --> 53:05.636 align:middle
No one would say that number
is the touchstone or that is,

53:06.106 --> 53:07.236 align:middle
you know, maximum employment.

53:07.236 --> 53:08.986 align:middle
I would just say, you
asked about 3½ percent.

53:09.266 --> 53:13.546 align:middle
A 3½ percent unemployment
rate showed, you know,

53:14.096 --> 53:16.256 align:middle
gains being shared very widely

53:16.256 --> 53:18.696 align:middle
across the income spectrum-in
fact, going more to people

53:18.696 --> 53:19.726 align:middle
at the bottom end
of the spectrum.

53:19.906 --> 53:23.216 align:middle
It showed labor force
participation coming up as-up

53:23.216 --> 53:27.396 align:middle
above many estimates of its
trend, as people who'd been

53:27.396 --> 53:29.006 align:middle
out of the labor force
were being pulled

53:29.056 --> 53:30.806 align:middle
into a tight job market.

53:31.116 --> 53:34.076 align:middle
There's a lot to like about
a tough-a tight job market,

53:34.346 --> 53:36.726 align:middle
particularly in a world where
we didn't see inflation.

53:37.286 --> 53:38.926 align:middle
So, yes, we'd love
to get back to that.

53:38.926 --> 53:41.426 align:middle
I mean, I would say, we
would like to get back-rather

53:41.426 --> 53:43.696 align:middle
than to a particular
number, we'd like to get back

53:44.316 --> 53:47.596 align:middle
to a strong labor market
where wages are moving up,

53:48.216 --> 53:49.846 align:middle
where people can find work,

53:50.026 --> 53:52.446 align:middle
where labor force participation
is holding up nicely.

53:52.996 --> 53:54.956 align:middle
That's what we'd really
love to get back to.

53:54.956 --> 53:58.406 align:middle
Now, of course we would-we
need inflation to perform

53:58.406 --> 54:00.986 align:middle
in line with, with
our framework.

54:01.496 --> 54:05.276 align:middle
But the good news is, we
think we can have quite low

54:05.276 --> 54:07.216 align:middle
unemployment without
raising troubling inflation.

54:07.546 --> 54:07.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

54:10.526 --> 54:10.996 align:middle
Thank you.

54:10.996 --> 54:12.656 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer.

54:12.656 --> 54:13.926 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

54:14.426 --> 54:17.066 align:middle
Hi, Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

54:17.066 --> 54:20.086 align:middle
Chair Powell, I want to follow
up on the wealth gap issue.

54:20.086 --> 54:22.306 align:middle
I know you were-that
you have limited tools.

54:22.306 --> 54:26.206 align:middle
But are there things that you
can do, possibly in the area

54:26.206 --> 54:28.436 align:middle
of research and maybe
expand your research

54:28.436 --> 54:30.426 align:middle
on racial economic gaps?

54:32.576 --> 54:33.206 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:33.596 --> 54:41.316 align:middle
You know, we do-we are gifted
with a, a substantial group

54:41.316 --> 54:43.896 align:middle
of researchers who really
cover the waterfront.

54:43.896 --> 54:47.036 align:middle
And we do a significant
amount of research

54:47.086 --> 54:51.146 align:middle
on racial disparities
in-across multiple variables,

54:51.146 --> 54:53.736 align:middle
including wealth,
as you asked about.

54:54.016 --> 54:55.256 align:middle
So we do that.

54:55.256 --> 54:59.706 align:middle
And we also-remember, we
have our Division of Consumer

54:59.706 --> 55:01.426 align:middle
and Community Affairs,
which is present

55:01.426 --> 55:03.086 align:middle
in communities around
the country.

55:03.086 --> 55:05.706 align:middle
And the Reserve Banks all
have very active community

55:05.706 --> 55:06.456 align:middle
affairs groups.

55:06.776 --> 55:09.246 align:middle
They're present in
communities around the country.

55:09.246 --> 55:13.186 align:middle
So it wasn't just the
Fed-Fed Listens events.

55:13.676 --> 55:16.446 align:middle
It's more, just, over a
long period of time we,

55:16.486 --> 55:20.606 align:middle
we are in contact with
people in those communities

55:20.606 --> 55:22.816 align:middle
to understand their
experience of the economy.

55:23.206 --> 55:25.366 align:middle
We serve all Americans,
and we know that.

55:25.736 --> 55:29.216 align:middle
And we're going to use our
tools to, to reflect that fact.

55:29.346 --> 55:30.786 align:middle
So the answer is "yes."

55:30.786 --> 55:33.726 align:middle
We do quite a bit of research,
and I suppose we could do more,

55:33.726 --> 55:35.006 align:middle
but we really do a lot.

55:35.006 --> 55:38.826 align:middle
And we contribute to those
fields and those assessments of,

55:39.576 --> 55:41.956 align:middle
of the state of the economy.

55:41.956 --> 55:45.166 align:middle
And we do that not just because
it's interesting and important,

55:45.166 --> 55:46.676 align:middle
but because it's
important for the economy

55:46.676 --> 55:47.936 align:middle
and important for our mandate.

55:48.516 --> 55:50.506 align:middle
We are assigned "maximum
employment."

55:50.656 --> 55:51.646 align:middle
Now, what does that mean?

55:51.746 --> 55:52.706 align:middle
As I mentioned earlier,

55:52.706 --> 55:56.546 align:middle
it doesn't mean a particular
headline unemployment number.

55:57.096 --> 56:00.506 align:middle
What it means is
maximum employment.

56:00.506 --> 56:03.406 align:middle
So, you know, we look
at-look at that in many,

56:03.406 --> 56:04.706 align:middle
many different variables,

56:05.016 --> 56:08.936 align:middle
and we ask ourselves whether
those variables are-those labor

56:08.936 --> 56:11.676 align:middle
market conditions are
consistent with our assessments

56:11.676 --> 56:13.736 align:middle
of what would constitute
maximum employment.

56:13.946 --> 56:16.006 align:middle
And that would include
all of the things

56:16.006 --> 56:16.636 align:middle
that we're talking about.

56:16.636 --> 56:17.286 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

56:21.466 --> 56:21.946 align:middle
Thank you.

56:21.946 --> 56:22.576 align:middle
Greg Robb.

56:22.576 --> 56:23.246 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

56:23.676 --> 56:26.926 align:middle
Hi, Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

56:26.926 --> 56:27.686 align:middle
Thanks for this.

56:27.686 --> 56:32.016 align:middle
I want to go back to the new
forward guidance that you have.

56:32.016 --> 56:33.496 align:middle
And you said that it's powerful,

56:34.436 --> 56:38.016 align:middle
but you've already have two
dissent-two dissenting voters

56:38.016 --> 56:39.636 align:middle
on it.

56:39.636 --> 56:42.456 align:middle
And I was wondering if
there's other, other people

56:42.456 --> 56:44.096 align:middle
who argued against it.

56:44.096 --> 56:46.416 align:middle
And what do you say to
the two who dissented?

56:46.416 --> 56:50.936 align:middle
It looks like President Kashkari
wanted a simpler forward

56:50.936 --> 56:53.296 align:middle
guidance, and President
Kaplan thought

56:53.296 --> 56:57.436 align:middle
that the current guidance
you have was fine for now.

56:57.436 --> 57:00.966 align:middle
So, like, how did you argue back
on those, on those arguments?

57:00.966 --> 57:01.376 align:middle
Thank you.

57:01.446 --> 57:02.216 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

57:02.446 --> 57:05.666 align:middle
I wouldn't-I don't want to
comment particularly on,

57:05.706 --> 57:07.016 align:middle
on the two dissenters, but they,

57:07.016 --> 57:09.346 align:middle
they consent-they
dissented from, of course,

57:09.346 --> 57:11.306 align:middle
different perspectives, and,
and that should be clear.

57:11.306 --> 57:15.926 align:middle
That's their-they're not-they're
sort of on, on two sides

57:16.026 --> 57:17.486 align:middle
of the, of the discussion.

57:18.036 --> 57:20.446 align:middle
But I would say this: I am
blessed with having a Committee

57:20.446 --> 57:23.756 align:middle
of highly thoughtful people who
bring diverse life experiences

57:23.756 --> 57:25.506 align:middle
and diverse careers,
and, of course,

57:25.546 --> 57:27.156 align:middle
diverse views to our work.

57:27.486 --> 57:28.746 align:middle
And I wouldn't have
it any other way.

57:28.886 --> 57:29.296 align:middle
I wouldn't.

57:29.696 --> 57:32.126 align:middle
So, so I would just say,

57:32.676 --> 57:38.286 align:middle
the right-in our discussions
the last couple of days,

57:38.286 --> 57:40.026 align:middle
the whole Committee, everybody

57:40.026 --> 57:44.246 align:middle
on the Committee is very
supportive of the Statement

57:44.246 --> 57:46.876 align:middle
on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary
Policy Strategy and what's

57:46.876 --> 57:48.976 align:middle
in there-very, very
broad support,

57:49.176 --> 57:50.366 align:middle
unanimous support for that.

57:50.696 --> 57:54.016 align:middle
Everyone sees the, the changes
in the underlying economy

57:54.016 --> 57:55.406 align:middle
and sees, in their own way,

57:55.866 --> 58:00.726 align:middle
the need to address those-and
including the changes we made

58:00.726 --> 58:04.986 align:middle
to the employment mandate and
to inflation so that we're now

58:04.986 --> 58:07.036 align:middle
at flexible average
inflation targeting.

58:07.326 --> 58:09.126 align:middle
Of course, there would
be-this is-you know,

58:09.126 --> 58:13.256 align:middle
we're the first major central
bank to adopt this framework.

58:14.136 --> 58:19.406 align:middle
There's no cookbook, and we-you
know, this is the first guidance

58:19.406 --> 58:20.286 align:middle
under our new framework.

58:20.286 --> 58:22.346 align:middle
So, of course there would
be a wide range of views,

58:22.346 --> 58:23.316 align:middle
and you would expect that.

58:23.316 --> 58:24.826 align:middle
And it's actually
a healthy thing.

58:25.116 --> 58:26.706 align:middle
So I welcome that discussion.

58:27.036 --> 58:30.486 align:middle
I would also say this: You know,
this is all about credibility,

58:30.486 --> 58:33.856 align:middle
and we understand perfectly that
we have to earn credibility.

58:33.856 --> 58:37.246 align:middle
This, this facility-this,
this framework has to-we have

58:37.246 --> 58:38.636 align:middle
to support it with our actions.

58:38.636 --> 58:42.046 align:middle
And I think today is a very
good first step in doing that.

58:42.176 --> 58:44.116 align:middle
It is strong, powerful guidance.

58:44.116 --> 58:47.126 align:middle
It is-it ties in very
nicely with the Statement

58:47.126 --> 58:49.326 align:middle
of [on] Longer-Run Goals and
Monetary Policy Strategy.

58:49.976 --> 58:52.176 align:middle
We had quite a robust
discussion, and there are,

58:52.176 --> 58:56.396 align:middle
there are different ideas on how
to do this, but I-that's just,

58:56.396 --> 58:59.716 align:middle
that's just the way it is
when you have a diverse group

58:59.716 --> 59:01.836 align:middle
of highly thoughtful
and effective people.

59:02.566 --> 59:05.406 align:middle
And so I'm pleased
with where it came out.

59:05.566 --> 59:06.006 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

59:08.626 --> 59:09.106 align:middle
Thank you.

59:09.106 --> 59:09.776 align:middle
Hannah Lang.

59:09.776 --> 59:10.076 align:middle
HANNAH LANG.

59:13.756 --> 59:14.856 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

59:14.856 --> 59:16.366 align:middle
Thanks so much for
taking my question.

59:16.436 --> 59:20.736 align:middle
I wanted to ask about
commercial real estate.

59:20.736 --> 59:23.666 align:middle
I, I know you had mentioned
before how you weren't sure

59:23.666 --> 59:26.746 align:middle
if there was a way the Fed
could support CRE borrowers.

59:26.746 --> 59:28.016 align:middle
But I was just wondering

59:28.016 --> 59:30.646 align:middle
if you had-if you've had any
other continuing discussions

59:30.646 --> 59:34.306 align:middle
on that and if there's
any potential way

59:34.306 --> 59:36.206 align:middle
that the Fed could
step in in that area.

59:36.336 --> 59:36.766 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

59:36.826 --> 59:39.346 align:middle
So thank you for that question.

59:39.346 --> 59:43.316 align:middle
You know, we've actually spent
quite a bit of time on this

59:43.316 --> 59:45.466 align:middle
as Secretary Mnuchin, I think,
mentioned the other day.

59:45.996 --> 59:47.976 align:middle
And I'll say just
a couple things.

59:48.026 --> 59:53.416 align:middle
First, you know, our facilities
are essentially always-they have

59:53.416 --> 59:55.946 align:middle
to be, under the
law-broad based and not

59:55.946 --> 59:57.926 align:middle
so much targeting
any single sector.

59:58.726 --> 01:00:02.186 align:middle
Also, it's important
to remember that CRE,

01:00:02.186 --> 01:00:05.066 align:middle
commercial real estate,
benefits from several

01:00:05.066 --> 01:00:06.536 align:middle
of our existing facilities.

01:00:06.606 --> 01:00:09.936 align:middle
So the TALF takes commercial
mortgage-backed securities

01:00:09.936 --> 01:00:12.306 align:middle
and SBA commercial
real estate deals.

01:00:12.306 --> 01:00:16.706 align:middle
And the New York Fed purchases
agency CMBSs directly.

01:00:17.026 --> 01:00:18.216 align:middle
In addition, I would say,

01:00:18.806 --> 01:00:24.316 align:middle
Main Street helps businesses
pay their rent, you know.

01:00:24.316 --> 01:00:27.326 align:middle
So we're, we're helping
real estate, you know,

01:00:28.426 --> 01:00:30.866 align:middle
in a number of other
ways-commercial real estate.

01:00:31.276 --> 01:00:33.486 align:middle
Also, CMBS issuance has resumed.

01:00:33.486 --> 01:00:35.386 align:middle
Spreads have tightened on CMBS.

01:00:35.796 --> 01:00:37.796 align:middle
There are a couple of issues.

01:00:37.916 --> 01:00:40.216 align:middle
One is just that
commercial properties

01:00:40.216 --> 01:00:43.316 align:middle
with CMBS loans often have
covenants-uniformly, I think,

01:00:43.316 --> 01:00:45.596 align:middle
have covenants that forbid
them to take on more debt.

01:00:46.046 --> 01:00:49.646 align:middle
So you have a situation, and
you have a situation where,

01:00:50.096 --> 01:00:53.586 align:middle
where you-without a legal change
or some kind of an innovation

01:00:53.586 --> 01:00:56.506 align:middle
that defies discovery so far,

01:00:56.766 --> 01:00:59.596 align:middle
you're-you have a hard
time providing mass relief

01:00:59.676 --> 01:01:02.036 align:middle
with regard to real
estate that's in,

01:01:02.146 --> 01:01:03.846 align:middle
in commercial mortgage-backed
securities.

01:01:04.466 --> 01:01:05.376 align:middle
So we're still working on it.

01:01:05.376 --> 01:01:06.206 align:middle
We're still looking.

01:01:06.206 --> 01:01:08.706 align:middle
I would say, it may be
that further support

01:01:08.706 --> 01:01:11.856 align:middle
for commercial real estate
will require further action

01:01:11.856 --> 01:01:13.126 align:middle
for Congress-from Congress.

01:01:13.476 --> 01:01:13.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

01:01:16.086 --> 01:01:17.816 align:middle
For the last question,
we'll go to Brian Cheung.

01:01:17.816 --> 01:01:18.286 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

01:01:18.286 --> 01:01:20.076 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

01:01:20.076 --> 01:01:23.136 align:middle
So it seems like a lot of the
new inflation framework is

01:01:23.136 --> 01:01:25.776 align:middle
about shaping inflation
expectations.

01:01:25.776 --> 01:01:26.756 align:middle
But the average American

01:01:26.756 --> 01:01:28.466 align:middle
who might be watching
this might be confused

01:01:28.466 --> 01:01:31.526 align:middle
as to why the Fed is
overshooting inflation.

01:01:31.526 --> 01:01:34.726 align:middle
So what's your explanation
to Main Street,

01:01:34.726 --> 01:01:37.466 align:middle
to average people what the
Fed is trying to do here

01:01:37.466 --> 01:01:41.216 align:middle
and what the outcome would
be for those on Main Street?

01:01:41.216 --> 01:01:42.156 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

01:01:42.156 --> 01:01:44.986 align:middle
That's a very, very important
question, and I actually spoke

01:01:44.986 --> 01:01:47.326 align:middle
about that in my Jackson Hole
remarks a couple weeks ago.

01:01:47.326 --> 01:01:48.956 align:middle
It's not intuitive to people.

01:01:48.956 --> 01:01:51.586 align:middle
It is intuitive that, that
high inflation is a bad thing.

01:01:51.806 --> 01:01:54.806 align:middle
It's less intuitive that
inflation can be too low.

01:01:55.496 --> 01:01:59.066 align:middle
And the way I would
explain it is,

01:01:59.636 --> 01:02:03.956 align:middle
is that inflation
that's too low will mean

01:02:03.956 --> 01:02:05.096 align:middle
that interest rates are lower.

01:02:05.546 --> 01:02:07.806 align:middle
There's an expectation of
future inflation that's built

01:02:07.806 --> 01:02:09.646 align:middle
into every interest rate, right?

01:02:09.646 --> 01:02:12.446 align:middle
And to the extent inflation
gets lower and lower and lower,

01:02:12.446 --> 01:02:13.796 align:middle
interest rates get
lower and lower.

01:02:13.796 --> 01:02:15.856 align:middle
And then the Fed will have
less room to cut rates

01:02:15.856 --> 01:02:16.876 align:middle
to support the economy.

01:02:17.366 --> 01:02:21.196 align:middle
And this isn't some idle,
you know, academic theory.

01:02:21.196 --> 01:02:23.026 align:middle
This is what's happening
all over the world.

01:02:23.056 --> 01:02:25.826 align:middle
If you, if you look at many,
many large jurisdictions

01:02:25.826 --> 01:02:28.206 align:middle
around the world, you are
seeing that phenomenon.

01:02:28.206 --> 01:02:31.846 align:middle
So we want inflation to be-we
want it to be 2 percent,

01:02:31.846 --> 01:02:34.396 align:middle
and we want it to
average 2 percent.

01:02:34.396 --> 01:02:36.166 align:middle
So if inflation averages
2 percent,

01:02:36.926 --> 01:02:38.226 align:middle
the public will expect that,

01:02:38.616 --> 01:02:40.576 align:middle
and that'll be what's
built into interest rates.

01:02:40.576 --> 01:02:41.766 align:middle
And that's just-that's
all we want.

01:02:41.766 --> 01:02:44.326 align:middle
So we're not looking
to have high inflation.

01:02:44.326 --> 01:02:46.356 align:middle
We just want inflation
to average 2 percent.

01:02:46.656 --> 01:02:50.686 align:middle
And that means that, you know,
in a downturn, these days,

01:02:50.686 --> 01:02:53.176 align:middle
what happens is, inflation,
as has happened now-it moves

01:02:53.176 --> 01:02:54.916 align:middle
down well below 2 percent.

01:02:55.346 --> 01:02:58.616 align:middle
And that means that we've said
for, for-we would like to see

01:02:58.616 --> 01:03:02.726 align:middle
and we will conduct policies
so that inflation moves,

01:03:03.206 --> 01:03:05.916 align:middle
for some time, moderately
above 2 percent.

01:03:06.436 --> 01:03:08.456 align:middle
So it won't be-these
won't be large overshoots,

01:03:08.456 --> 01:03:09.456 align:middle
and they won't be permanent

01:03:09.536 --> 01:03:12.636 align:middle
but to help anchor inflation
expectations at 2 percent.

01:03:13.486 --> 01:03:16.036 align:middle
So, yes, it's,

01:03:16.586 --> 01:03:19.046 align:middle
it's a challenging concept
for a lot of people.

01:03:19.046 --> 01:03:23.656 align:middle
But, nonetheless, the economic
importance of it is, is large.

01:03:24.296 --> 01:03:27.406 align:middle
And, you know, those are the
people we're serving, and,

01:03:27.686 --> 01:03:29.986 align:middle
you know, we serve them best

01:03:30.206 --> 01:03:32.756 align:middle
if we can actually achieve
average 2 percent inflation,

01:03:32.756 --> 01:03:33.176 align:middle
we believe.

01:03:33.176 --> 01:03:34.496 align:middle
And that's why we
changed our framework.

01:03:34.606 --> 01:03:35.106 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

01:03:37.086 --> 01:03:38.386 align:middle
Thank you very much.

01:03:38.386 --> 01:03:38.646 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

01:03:40.406 --> 01:03:41.086 align:middle
Thanks very much.

